//! sched — preemptive kernel threads on one CPU (px07, M-PX2's
//! scheduler).
//!
//! **A thread** is a record in boot/sched.S's `pax_threads` (64 slots since px24, 16 before;
//! module state holds scalars only, [mem.static.3]), a stack, and, while
//! it is not running, the frame it resumes from. Slot 0 is the kernel's
//! own first context (kmain on Limine's stack), adopted by `start` as
//! thread 0; slot 1 is the idle thread (boot/sched.S's `pax_idle`, HLT
//! with IF set), run only when no other thread is ready and never
//! queued; slots 2-63 are spawned threads. Ids are 0 (main), 1 (idle),
//! then 2, 3, … in spawn order, never reused; slots are.
//!
//! **A stack** (slots 1-63): slot s owns the 64 KiB of virtual space at
//! `STACKS + s * SPAN`, PML4 slot 416 (0xffffd00000000000, free since
//! px03: PAX maps 256, 320, 352 and 511). Its low 48 KiB are never
//! mapped — the guard — and its high 16 KiB are four frames from
//! `frames.alloc`, mapped read-write no-execute by `paging.map`. A
//! thread that runs off its stack writes into the guard and faults;
//! the CPU's attempt to push the page fault's frame on that same stack
//! faults again, which is a double fault (Intel SDM vol. 3A §6.15,
//! interrupt 8, Table 6-5), delivered on IST1 (kernel/idt). The fatal
//! path in kernel/interrupts asks `guard_of` whether CR2 lies in a
//! thread's guard and panics `stack overflow` by name.
//!
//! **A switch** is boot/isr.S's common path resuming the frame
//! `pax_interrupt` returns (boot/sched.S explains why a saved frame is
//! enough). Two ways in:
//! - `preempt(f)`, from the timer (vector 32, after the tick and its
//!   EOI): every sleeper whose tick has come is made ready (in slot
//!   order); then the current thread keeps the CPU if its quantum (two
//!   ticks) is not used up or no other thread is ready — except the
//!   idle thread, which gives way the moment anything is ready;
//!   otherwise its frame is saved, it goes to the run queue's tail and
//!   the head runs.
//! - `switched(f)`, from `pax_switch` (vector 0x81, boot/sched.S), after
//!   the current thread has set its own state: `yield` (ready: to the
//!   tail), `sleep` (sleeping until a tick), `wait_all` (blocked until
//!   its threads have exited), `exit` (dead). Its frame is saved and the
//!   head runs.
//! The run queue is FIFO, linked through each record's `next` (slot + 1;
//! 0 ends it), its ends in module `var`s. A quantum is two ticks (~20
//! ms): a thread switched in voluntarily, part-way through a tick, still
//! gets at least one whole tick. Every queue and record change is made
//! with IF clear (a handler, or `sync.irq_save`).
//!
//! **A new thread's first frame** sits at its stack's top: RIP
//! `pax_thread_entry`, CS 0x08, RFLAGS 0x202 (IF), RSP the top, SS 0x10,
//! %r12 the body's address, %r13 its argument (boot/sched.S calls the
//! body, then `pax_thread_exit`). A body is an `export fn NAME(arg: u64)`
//! whose address the caller names with `extern "c" let NAME: *u8`: wolf
//! has no C function-pointer value (wolf-lang#520), and the `extern`
//! must sit in another module than the export (E0302 in the same one).
//!
//! **Exit** marks the thread dead and switches away for good; the code
//! that switched away is running on the dead thread's stack, so the
//! stack is reclaimed later, from another thread: `create` and `wait_all`
//! reap every dead thread first (unmap its four pages, INVLPG through
//! `paging.unmap`, give the frames back, free the slot). Reaping is
//! never done in a handler, so `frames` and `paging` are only ever
//! entered from thread context with IF clear.
//!
//! **User threads** (px09): a thread may own an address space, a PML4
//! from `paging.new_space` (its `cr3` field; 0 for a kernel thread,
//! which runs on the kernel's PML4) and a user program (`prog`,
//! kernel/user's number plus one). `run_next` makes the switched-in
//! thread's space live (MOV to CR3 only when it differs from the live
//! one) and points the TSS's RSP0 (kernel/gdt) and SYSCALL's stack word
//! (boot/user.S's `pax_syscall_state` word 0) at the top of its kernel
//! stack, so an interrupt, exception or system call from ring 3 lands
//! on the stack of the thread that was running. A thread ended from a
//! handler (a system call's exit, a fault in ring 3: `end_current`)
//! is dead at once and the handler resumes the next thread's frame;
//! `reap` gives back its address space with its stack, in thread
//! context, as every reap is.
//!
//! **ELF processes** (px10): a user thread may also carry a program
//! loaded from the initramfs (kernel/user's `exec`): its entry point,
//! its initial stack pointer (the psABI's argc/argv/envp/auxv block) and
//! its name (a pointer into the initramfs and a length), so `who` lines
//! name it and `pax_user_thread` enters it where the loader said. And
//! every switch between two threads moves the FPU state with them
//! (`fpu.switch` in `run_next`; a new thread's area starts as the
//! template, `fpu.reset`), once kernel/fpu has started.
//!
//! **Linux processes** (px12): a thread also carries its FS base
//! (`arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS)`, glibc's thread pointer), written to
//! IA32_FS_BASE (MSR 0xC0000100) at every switch in (`enter_space`),
//! so each thread finds its own; CR4.FSGSBASE stays clear, so ring 3
//! cannot change FS behind the kernel (WRFSBASE is #UD). And each slot
//! owns a process area in boot/sched.S's `pax_procs` (2048 bytes, the
//! words kernel/vm, kernel/files and kernel/user keep for the process
//! in it), zeroed whenever the slot is cleared: a zeroed area is a fresh
//! process (no break yet, the console on descriptors 0-2). A system
//! call may also put its thread to sleep (`sleep_current`, for
//! `clock_nanosleep`): its frame is saved with the result already in
//! %rax, and the timer wakes it as it wakes `sleep`.
//!
//! **Console input** (px13): a system call may also wait for what a
//! person types (`block_input`, for `read` on the console with nothing
//! ready): its frame is saved, rewound by kernel/user to re-run the
//! `syscall`, and the console's interrupt makes it ready
//! (`wake_input`) when a read would answer; `kick` switches to it at
//! once when the CPU was idle.
//!
//! **Processes** (px14): a user thread is a process with a Linux pid of
//! its own (1 for the first, then 2, 3, … never reused in a boot; kernel
//! threads have none) and its parent's (0 when the kernel started it).
//! `clone_current` makes a child that borrows the caller's address space
//! (`owns` 0: nothing frees a space a process only borrows) and
//! `vfork_wait` suspends the caller (VFORK) until the child execs
//! (`exec_current`, which makes the new space its own and gives the old
//! one back or hands it back) or ends. An ending process (`finish`)
//! resumes a vfork parent it holds, gives its children to pid 1 (or to
//! nobody when it is pid 1 or pid 1 has ended), and, when it has a
//! parent, is kept as a ZOMBIE once reaped (its stack and space given
//! back, its pid and status word kept) and wakes the parent if it is in
//! wait4 (WAITING); `child_ended` and `zombie` are the parent's side.
//! Reaping may run in a system call too (wait4): never on the dead
//! thread's own stack, always with IF clear.
//!
//! **Pipes** (px17): a system call may also wait for a pipe or a socket
//! (`block_pipe`, for a read of an empty one or a write to a full one):
//! its frame is saved, rewound by kernel/user to re-run the `syscall`,
//! and every change to any pipe (bytes in or out, an end closed) makes
//! every such thread ready (`wake_pipes`), each to run its call again
//! and wait again if its pipe still cannot answer. And `clone_current`
//! also makes a fork's child (px17): a copy of the caller's address
//! space (kernel/paging's `copy_user`), its own, which it frees when it
//! execs or ends.
//!
//! **The network thread** (px20): a kernel thread may also wait for the
//! network device (`block_net`, in thread context like `sleep`); the
//! device's interrupt makes it ready (`wake_net`) and `kick` runs it
//! at once when the CPU was idle.
//!
//! **Threads** (px26): a process is a GROUP of slots with one leader.
//! A thread's `lead` names its leader's slot, whose process area and
//! address space the whole group uses (`proc_area` follows it), and its
//! `tgid` is the process's pid; its own `pid` is its tid. `clone_thread`
//! makes one in the caller's group. `end_thread` ends one thread (a
//! leader's slot is HELD until its last thread has ended: it carries the
//! pid, the area and the space); `end_with` ends the process, every
//! thread at once, wherever each was waiting or queued, and leaves the
//! leader's slot the zombie a parent waits for; `exec_alone` ends the
//! others before an `execve` and makes the caller the leader. A parent
//! is a process: any of its threads may wait for a child.
//!
//! **Futexes** (px26): a thread may wait on a futex (`futex_wait`, state
//! FUTEX): its slot holds the key, the bitset and its place in line;
//! `futex_wake` readies the longest-waiting matches with 0 as their
//! call's result; a deadline leaves -ETIMEDOUT (`wake_sleepers`).
//!
//! **Signals** (px26): each slot holds the signals pending for its
//! thread and, a leader's, for its process (`raise_thread`,
//! `raise_group`), with a word per signal for where it came from.
//! `take_signal` hands kernel/signal the running thread's next one;
//! `poke` readies a thread waiting in a system call when a signal
//! arrives that will run a handler or end its process, and each wait
//! says how it is to be ended (`W_*`, read through `self_wait`).
//!
//! **Regions** (px21a): wolf's freestanding runtime keeps one
//! ambient-region slot, and every switch moves it with the thread:
//! `run_next` stores `heap.ambient()` in the outgoing thread's record
//! and hands the incoming thread's saved value (0, the root, for a
//! thread that has not run) to `heap.set_ambient`. So a thread may hold
//! a `region` open across a yield, a preemption or a wait and its
//! allocations land in its own region. A return to ring 3 switches no
//! thread and needs nothing saved. What is still refused by rule: a
//! system call's handler leaves no region open where it gives up its
//! kernel stack for good (`sleep_current`, `block_input`,
//! `block_pipe`, `vfork_wait`, `wait_child`, `futex_wait`,
//! `yield_current`, `end_thread`, `end_with`, `execve`: the
//! code after such a wait never runs, so the region would never be
//! left), and no interrupt handler allocates.
//!
//! The record's fields are read and written as words at their
//! `offset_of`, volatile (`field`/`put_field`): wolf 0.2.26 stores a
//! field of a raw element in place (wolf-lang#577), but as an ordinary
//! access, which the compiler may merge or move; these words are
//! polled across switches and written by other threads. Written from the Intel SDM vol. 3A §6.12.1, §6.14.2,
//! §6.14.3, §6.15 (interrupt 8, Table 6-5), §4.7 (page-fault CR2) and
//! the AMD64 APM vol. 2 §8.9.3; the design (a FIFO run queue, a frame
//! saved on the thread's own stack, deferred reaping) is the textbook
//! one, consulted from no kernel's source. No Linux source.

use fpu
use frames
use gdt
use heap
use log
use paging
use panic
use speaker
use sync
use timer

extern "c" fn pax_switch()
extern "c" fn pax_wrmsr(msr: u64, v: u64)

extern "c" let pax_threads: *u64
extern "c" let pax_sched_state: *u64
extern "c" let pax_thread_entry: *u8
extern "c" let pax_idle: *u8
extern "c" let pax_syscall_state: *u64
extern "c" let pax_procs: *u64
extern "c" let pax_siginfo: *u64

/// One thread's record (boot/sched.S: 64 of them, 512 bytes each; 72
/// before px09 added `cr3` and `prog`, 88 before px10's four, 120
/// before px12's `fs`, 128 bytes a slot before px14's seven; px21a's
/// `amb` is the 24th word).
#[repr(c)]
struct Thread {
    frame: u64, // the saved frame's address; 0 while running
    state: u64, // FREE … DEAD
    wake: u64, // the tick a sleeper wakes at
    next: u64, // the run queue's link: slot + 1, 0 ends it
    id: u64, // 0 main, 1 idle, then 2, 3, …
    stack_lo: u64, // the lowest mapped stack byte (0 for main)
    stack_hi: u64, // the first byte past the stack
    runs: u64, // times switched in
    yields: u64, // calls of `yield`
    cr3: u64, // px09: the thread's PML4 (physical); 0 the kernel's
    prog: u64, // px09: kernel/user's program number + 1; 0 a kernel thread
    entry: u64, // px10: an ELF process's entry point (0 otherwise)
    usp: u64, // px10: its initial user stack pointer
    name: u64, // px10: its name's first byte (in the initramfs)
    name_len: u64, // px10: its name's length
    fs: u64, // px12: its FS base (IA32_FS_BASE while it runs)
    pid: u64, // px14: its Linux process id (0 for a kernel thread)
    ppid: u64, // px14: its parent's pid (0: started by the kernel)
    status: u64, // px14: its wait status word, once it has ended
    owns: u64, // px14: 1 when `cr3` is its own (0: borrowed by a vfork child)
    vparent: u64, // px14: slot + 1 of the vfork parent it holds suspended; 0 none
    zombie: u64, // px14: 1 when a parent will wait for it (the slot outlives the reap)
    sigmask: u64, // px14: its blocked signals (rt_sigprocmask)
    amb: u64, // px21a: its ambient region while it is switched out; 0 the root
    tgid: u64, // px26: its process's pid (`pid` is the thread's own id, the tid)
    lead: u64, // px26: the slot of its group's leader, whose area and space the group shares
    threads: u64, // px26: a leader's count of its group's threads that have not ended
    gone: u64, // px26: 1 for a leader that has ended as a thread while its group lives
    ctid: u64, // px26: where 0 is written and a futex woken when it ends (set_tid_address)
    pend: u64, // px26: the signals pending for this thread (bit n - 1 for signal n)
    gpend: u64, // px26: a leader's: the signals pending for the process
    fkey: u64, // px26: the futex it waits on (the word's physical address)
    fbits: u64, // px26: that wait's bitset
    fseq: u64, // px26: that wait's place in line
    rst: u64, // px26: how its system call is waiting (W_*), for a signal that ends the wait
    rarg: u64, // px26: that wait's argument (a sleep's `rem` pointer)
    done: u64, // px26: bytes a waiting pipe write has written (the area's word before threads)
    name0: u64, // px26: PR_SET_NAME's sixteen bytes
    name1: u64,
    until: u64, // px24: the tick its waiting call gives up at (poll, epoll_wait, a receive with a timeout); 0 none
    ss_sp: u64, // px24: sigaltstack: the alternate signal stack's lowest byte
    ss_size: u64, // px24: its size; 0 none (SS_DISABLE)
    pgid: u64, // px48: a leader's: its process's group
    sid: u64, // px48: a leader's: its process's session
    ctty: u64, // px48: a leader's: 1 when the console is its process's controlling terminal (while its session holds the console)
    execd: u64, // px48: a leader's: 1 once its process has called execve since its fork (setpgid's EACCES)
    stopped: u64, // px48: a leader's: 1 while its process is stopped
    wstop: u64, // px48: a leader's: a stop no wait has reported yet: the signal (0 none)
    wcont: u64, // px48: a leader's: 1 while a continue no wait has reported yet
    ssold: u64, // px48: the mask `rt_sigsuspend` replaced, while the thread is suspended in it
}

/// How many slots there are (px14: `clone_current` and `child_ended`
/// answer SLOTS for none).
pub const SLOTS: u64 = 64 // px24: sixteen before; wolf's hosted runtime runs a thread a parked task
const MAIN: u64 = 0
const IDLE: u64 = 1
const FIRST: u64 = 2 // the first spawnable slot

const FREE: u64 = 0
const READY: u64 = 1
const RUNNING: u64 = 2
const SLEEPING: u64 = 3
const BLOCKED: u64 = 4
const DEAD: u64 = 5
const INPUT: u64 = 6 // px13: waiting for console input (kernel/console)
const ZOMBIE: u64 = 7 // px14: ended, its stack and space given back, its status kept for its parent
const VFORK: u64 = 8 // px14: a parent suspended until its vfork child execs or ends
const WAITING: u64 = 9 // px14: in wait4/waitid until a child ends
const PIPE: u64 = 10 // px17: in a read or write of a pipe or socket that must wait
const NET: u64 = 11 // px20: the network thread, until the device's interrupt
const FUTEX: u64 = 12 // px26: in a futex wait
const HELD: u64 = 13 // px26: a leader that has ended while its group's threads live on: no stack, never run, its slot the group's
const STOPPED: u64 = 14 // px48: a thread of a stopped process, parked with its frame until the process is continued (or killed)
const SUSPEND: u64 = 15 // px48: in rt_sigsuspend until a signal that runs a handler or ends the process

/// px26: how a thread's system call is waiting, read when a signal ends
/// the wait (kernel/signal): `W_REWOUND` the frame is rewound to run the
/// call again (a read of the console or a pipe, a pipe write, wait4,
/// waitid: run again under SA_RESTART, else -EINTR); `W_SLEEP` a sleep,
/// its result already in the frame (-EINTR always); `W_FUTEX` a futex
/// wait with no timeout, its result in the frame (run again under
/// SA_RESTART, else -EINTR); `W_TIMED` a futex wait with a deadline,
/// its result in the frame (-EINTR always); `W_TIMED_REWOUND` a
/// receive with a timeout, its frame rewound (-EINTR always).
pub const W_NONE: u64 = 0
pub const W_REWOUND: u64 = 1
pub const W_SLEEP: u64 = 2
pub const W_FUTEX: u64 = 3
pub const W_TIMED: u64 = 4
pub const W_TIMED_REWOUND: u64 = 5
/// px48: `rt_sigsuspend`: -EINTR in the frame, and the mask it replaced
/// put back by the handler's frame (px49: a thread out of it with no
/// handler run waits again, `resuspend`).
pub const W_SUSPEND: u64 = 6

const STACKS: u64 = 0xffffd00000000000 // PML4 slot 416
const SPAN: u64 = 65536 // each slot's virtual space
const GUARD: u64 = 49152 // its low part, never mapped
const STACK: u64 = 16384 // its high part, mapped
const PAGE: u64 = 4096
const PAGES: u64 = 4
const PROC_AREA: u64 = 40960 // px12: each slot's bytes of boot/sched.S's pax_procs (px34: 1024 descriptors and 256 reservations; 8192 before, 4096 before px24, 2048 before px14)
const FS_BASE: u64 = 0xc0000100 // px12: IA32_FS_BASE (Intel SDM vol. 4)

const QUANTUM: u64 = 2 // ticks

/// The `prog` an ELF process carries (px10): past every kernel/user
/// blob's number + 1.
pub const ELF_PROG: u64 = 0x100

// boot/isr.S's frame, as word indices (kernel/interrupts' Frame: r15 r14
// r13 r12 … vector error rip cs rflags rsp ss).
const FRAME_WORDS: u64 = 22
const W_R13: u64 = 2
const W_R12: u64 = 3
const W_RAX: u64 = 14 // px14: a system call's result
const W_RIP: u64 = 17
const W_CS: u64 = 18
const W_RFLAGS: u64 = 19
const W_RSP: u64 = 20
const W_SS: u64 = 21
const KERNEL_CS: u64 = 0x08
const KERNEL_SS: u64 = 0x10
const RFLAGS_IF: u64 = 0x202 // IF and the always-one bit 1

// The scheduler's scalars, as words of boot/sched.S's `pax_sched_state`
// read and written volatile — NOT module `var`s (#598): wolf 0.2.24's
// compiled tiers forward a module var's value (and a store through an
// `extern "c" let` pointer) across a call that writes it
// (wolf-lang#598), and every switch is such a call — `pax_switch`
// returns after the timer and other threads have rewritten the run
// queue, the current slot and the tick count. A volatile access is
// never forwarded or merged ([mem.unsafe.volatile]), so each read here
// is the word as it is now.
const S_STARTED: u64 = 0
const S_CURRENT: u64 = 1 // the running thread's slot
const S_HEAD: u64 = 2 // slot + 1; 0 when the queue is empty
const S_TAIL: u64 = 3
const S_SLICE: u64 = 4 // the tick the current thread was switched in at
const S_NEXT_ID: u64 = 5
const S_LIVE: u64 = 6 // created threads not yet exited
const S_WAITER: u64 = 7 // slot + 1 of the thread in wait_all; 0 none
const S_PML4: u64 = 8
const S_NEXT_PID: u64 = 9 // px14: the next Linux pid (1 first)
const S_FLOOR: u64 = 10 // px22: the live count the waiter waits for (0: `wait_all`'s)
const S_FSEQ: u64 = 11 // px26: the next futex waiter's place in line
const S_THREADS: u64 = 12 // px26: threads made by clone since boot
const S_SIGNALS: u64 = 13 // px26: signals delivered to a handler since boot
const S_TTY_ON: u64 = 14 // px48: 1 once the console is a terminal (kernel/console's `start`)
const S_TTY_SID: u64 = 15 // px48: the session the console is the controlling terminal of; 0 none
const S_TTY_PGRP: u64 = 16 // px48: the console's foreground process group; 0 none

const W_RAX_OFF: u64 = 112 // the frame's %rax (word 14)
const NEG_ETIMEDOUT: u64 = 0xffffffffffffff92 // -110
const NSIG: u64 = 64
const SIG_WORDS: u64 = 256 // the area's signal actions, four words a signal (kernel/process)
const SIG_DFL: u64 = 0
const SIG_IGN: u64 = 1
const SIGKILL: u64 = 9
const SIGCHLD: u64 = 17
const SIGCONT: u64 = 18
const SIGSTOP: u64 = 19
const SIGTSTP: u64 = 20
const SIGTTIN: u64 = 21
const SIGTTOU: u64 = 22
const SIGURG: u64 = 23
const SIGWINCH: u64 = 28

/// Field `off` of slot `s`'s record. Volatile: threads poll their own
/// record while the timer rewrites it.
fn field(s: u64, off: int) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: s < SLOTS, so the word is inside boot/sched.S's table.
    unsafe { ((pax_threads as u64 + s * size_of(Thread) as u64 + off as u64) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn put_field(s: u64, off: int, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `get`.
    unsafe { ((pax_threads as u64 + s * size_of(Thread) as u64 + off as u64) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

/// Scheduler word `i` (S_*), read volatile (#598).
fn sv(i: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: i < 32, inside boot/sched.S's 256-byte pax_sched_state (px48; 128 before).
    unsafe { ((pax_sched_state as u64 + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

/// Write scheduler word `i` (#598).
fn put_sv(i: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `sv`.
    unsafe { ((pax_sched_state as u64 + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn cur() -> u64 {
    sv(S_CURRENT) // #598: reread after every switch
}

/// Slot `s`'s virtual space: [base, base + SPAN).
fn base_of(s: u64) -> u64 {
    STACKS + s * SPAN
}

fn enqueue(s: u64) {
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, next), 0)
    let t = sv(S_TAIL) // #598
    if t == 0 {
        put_sv(S_HEAD, s + 1)
    } else {
        put_field(t - 1, offset_of(Thread, next), s + 1)
    }
    put_sv(S_TAIL, s + 1)
}

/// The queue's head (slot + 1), removed; 0 when it is empty.
fn dequeue() -> u64 {
    let h = sv(S_HEAD) // #598
    if h == 0 {
        return 0
    }
    let s = h - 1
    let n = field(s, offset_of(Thread, next))
    put_sv(S_HEAD, n)
    if n == 0 {
        put_sv(S_TAIL, 0)
    }
    s + 1
}

/// Make every sleeper whose tick has come ready, in slot order.
fn wake_sleepers() {
    let now = timer.ticks()
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        let st = field(s, offset_of(Thread, state))
        let at = field(s, offset_of(Thread, wake))
        // px22: a pipe or socket wait with a deadline (`at` not 0) ends
        // at its tick too.
        // px26: and a futex wait with one (its -ETIMEDOUT is in its frame).
        if (st == SLEEPING || ((st == PIPE || st == FUTEX) && at != 0)) && at <= now {
            if st != PIPE {
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE) // the wait is over: no signal ends it now
            }
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
            enqueue(s)
        }
        s += 1
    }
    let w = field(MAIN, offset_of(Thread, state))
    if w == SLEEPING && field(MAIN, offset_of(Thread, wake)) <= now {
        put_field(MAIN, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
        enqueue(MAIN)
    }
}

/// Slot `s`'s address space made live, and its kernel stack's top made
/// the stack ring 3 enters on (px09): the TSS's RSP0 and SYSCALL's stack
/// word. IF clear (a handler, or `pax_switch`'s path).
fn enter_space(s: u64) {
    let c = field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3))
    let want = if c == 0 { sv(S_PML4) } else { c }
    if paging.live() != want {
        paging.load(want)
    }
    // # Safety: cpu.S's WRMSR of IA32_FS_BASE, an architectural MSR every
    // x86-64 CPU has; the kernel never uses FS, so only ring 3 sees it.
    unsafe { pax_wrmsr(FS_BASE, field(s, offset_of(Thread, fs))) }
    let hi = field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi))
    if hi != 0 {
        gdt.set_rsp0(hi)
        // # Safety: word 0 of boot/user.S's 64-byte pax_syscall_state.
        unsafe { (pax_syscall_state as *u64).write_volatile(hi) }
    }
}

/// Switch in the queue's head (the idle thread if it is empty): count
/// its run, start its quantum, make its space live, return its frame.
fn run_next() -> u64 {
    let from = cur()
    var n = dequeue()
    // px48: a thread of a stopped process runs nothing: it is parked
    // (STOPPED), its frame kept, until the process is continued.
    while n != 0 && parks(n - 1) {
        put_field(n - 1, offset_of(Thread, state), STOPPED)
        n = dequeue()
    }
    let s = if n == 0 { IDLE } else { n - 1 }
    if s != from {
        fpu.switch(from, s) // px10: a no-op before fpu.start
        // px21a: the ambient region goes with its thread. IF is clear.
        put_field(from, offset_of(Thread, amb), heap.ambient())
        heap.set_ambient(field(s, offset_of(Thread, amb)))
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), RUNNING)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, runs), field(s, offset_of(Thread, runs)) + 1)
    put_sv(S_CURRENT, s)
    put_sv(S_SLICE, timer.ticks())
    enter_space(s)
    let f = field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), 0)
    f
}

/// The timer's half of a switch (kernel/interrupts, vector 32, after
/// the tick). Returns the frame to resume: `f` itself, or another
/// thread's.
pub fn preempt(f: u64) -> u64 {
    if sv(S_STARTED) == 0 {
        return f
    }
    wake_sleepers()
    let c = cur()
    // #598: the queue's head is read after wake_sleepers wrote it.
    if sv(S_HEAD) == 0 {
        return f
    }
    if c != IDLE && timer.ticks() - sv(S_SLICE) < QUANTUM {
        return f
    }
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    if c != IDLE {
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
        enqueue(c)
    }
    run_next()
}

/// The voluntary half (kernel/interrupts, vector 0x81 from
/// `pax_switch`): the current thread has set its own state.
pub fn switched(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    let st = field(c, offset_of(Thread, state))
    if st == RUNNING {
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    }
    if (st == READY || st == RUNNING) && c != IDLE {
        enqueue(c)
    }
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// Map four fresh frames as slot `s`'s stack; record its bounds. px34:
/// false when the frames are not to be had (none kept), so a `clone`
/// answers -EAGAIN instead of the kernel panicking.
fn make_stack(s: u64) -> bool {
    let pml4 = sv(S_PML4)
    let lo = base_of(s) + GUARD
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < PAGES {
        let pa = frames.alloc() else 0
        if pa == 0 {
            while k > 0 {
                k -= 1
                let f = paging.unmap(pml4, lo + k * PAGE) else 0
                if f != 0 {
                    frames.free(f)
                }
            }
            return false
        }
        let n = paging.map(pml4, lo + k * PAGE, pa, paging.RW | paging.NX) else 99
        if n == 99 {
            panic.fail("sched: stack map refused at", lo + k * PAGE)
        }
        k += 1
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo), lo)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi), lo + STACK)
    true
}

/// A kernel thread's stack (one the kernel makes for itself, at boot or
/// for a program it starts): no frame for it is the kernel's end.
fn make_stack_or_die(s: u64) {
    if !make_stack(s) {
        panic.fail("sched: no frame for a stack, slot", s)
    }
}

/// px34: `clone_current`'s and `clone_thread`'s answer when no frame is
/// left for the new thread's kernel stack (kernel/process: -EAGAIN).
pub const NO_STACK: u64 = SLOTS + 1

/// Unmap slot `s`'s stack and give its frames back.
fn free_stack(s: u64) {
    let pml4 = sv(S_PML4)
    let lo = field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo))
    var k: u64 = 0
    while k < PAGES {
        let pa = paging.unmap(pml4, lo + k * PAGE) else 0
        if pa == 0 {
            panic.fail("sched: a dead thread's stack page was not mapped", lo + k * PAGE)
        }
        frames.free(pa)
        k += 1
    }
}

/// Write slot `s`'s first frame at its stack's top: it resumes in
/// `pax_thread_entry` with `entry` in %r12 and `arg` in %r13.
fn first_frame(s: u64, rip: u64, entry: u64, arg: u64) {
    let hi = field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi))
    let f = hi - FRAME_WORDS * 8
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < FRAME_WORDS {
        // # Safety: [f, hi) is inside the stack just mapped.
        unsafe { ((f + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
        i += 1
    }
    // # Safety: as above.
    unsafe {
        ((f + W_R12 * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(entry)
        ((f + W_R13 * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(arg)
        ((f + W_RIP * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(rip)
        ((f + W_CS * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(KERNEL_CS)
        ((f + W_RFLAGS * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(RFLAGS_IF)
        ((f + W_RSP * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(hi)
        ((f + W_SS * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(KERNEL_SS)
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
}

fn clear(s: u64) {
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), FREE)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, wake), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, next), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, id), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, runs), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, yields), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, prog), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, entry), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, usp), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name_len), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, fs), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ppid), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, status), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, owns), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, vparent), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, zombie), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, sigmask), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, amb), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, lead), s)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, threads), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, gone), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ctid), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pend), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, gpend), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, fkey), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, fbits), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, fseq), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, rst), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, rarg), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, done), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name0), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name1), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, until), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ss_sp), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ss_size), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pgid), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, sid), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ctty), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, execd), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stopped), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, wstop), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, wcont), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ssold), 0)
    let a = own_area(s)
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < PROC_AREA / 8 {
        // # Safety: slot s's area, inside boot/sched.S's pax_procs (s < SLOTS).
        unsafe { ((a + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
        i += 1
    }
}

/// Slot `s`'s own area, whichever group it is in (px26).
fn own_area(s: u64) -> u64 {
    if s >= SLOTS {
        panic.fail("sched: no process area for slot", s)
    }
    // # Safety: the address only; s < SLOTS keeps it inside pax_procs.
    unsafe { pax_procs as u64 + s * PROC_AREA }
}

/// Slot `s`'s process area (px12): PROC_AREA bytes of boot/sched.S's
/// `pax_procs`, zero for a fresh process. px26: the area of the thread's
/// process, which is its leader's slot's — every thread of a group
/// reads and writes the same descriptors, working directory, break and
/// signal actions.
pub fn proc_area(s: u64) -> u64 {
    if s >= SLOTS {
        panic.fail("sched: no process area for slot", s)
    }
    own_area(field(s, offset_of(Thread, lead)))
}

/// Thread `id`'s process area (px12; the id from `create_process`).
pub fn area_of(id: u64) -> u64 {
    proc_area(slot_of(id))
}

/// The running thread's slot (px12).
pub fn self_slot() -> u64 {
    cur()
}

/// The running thread's FS base (px12, `arch_prctl(ARCH_GET_FS)`).
pub fn self_fs() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, fs))
}

/// Set the running thread's FS base (px12, `arch_prctl(ARCH_SET_FS)`):
/// the record keeps it for every later switch in, and IA32_FS_BASE takes
/// it now. IF clear (a system call).
pub fn set_self_fs(v: u64) {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, fs), v)
    // # Safety: as in `enter_space`.
    unsafe { pax_wrmsr(FS_BASE, v) }
}

/// Put the running thread to sleep from a system call until tick `wake`
/// (px12, `clock_nanosleep`): its frame `f` (the result already in its
/// %rax) is saved, the thread sleeps as `sleep`'s do, and the frame
/// returned is the next thread's, for the handler to resume. IF clear.
pub fn sleep_current(f: u64, wake: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wake), wake)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), SLEEPING)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_SLEEP) // px26: a signal ends it with -EINTR
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px26: `sleep_current` for a relative sleep that reports the time left
/// when a signal ends it: `rem` is the user address of the timespec to
/// write (0 none), kept for kernel/signal (`self_wait`).
pub fn sleep_current_rem(f: u64, wake: u64, rem: u64) -> u64 {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, rarg), rem)
    sleep_current(f, wake)
}

/// Put the running thread to wait for console input from a system call
/// (px13, `read` on the console with nothing ready): its frame `f` is
/// saved as the caller left it (kernel/user has rewound it to re-run
/// the `syscall`), the thread waits until `wake_input`, and the frame
/// returned is the next thread's, for the handler to resume. IF clear.
pub fn block_input(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), INPUT)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_REWOUND) // px26
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// Make every thread waiting for console input ready (px13: from the
/// console's interrupt, when a read would answer). Returns how many.
/// IF clear.
pub fn wake_input() -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        // px24: a thread in `poll` or `epoll_wait` may be watching the
        // console too; it waits as a pipe's reader does (PIPE).
        let st = field(s, offset_of(Thread, state))
        if st == INPUT || st == PIPE {
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
            enqueue(s)
            n += 1
        }
        s += 1
    }
    n
}

/// After a device's interrupt (px13): when the idle thread was running
/// and a thread is now ready, switch to it at once rather than at the
/// next tick; otherwise resume `f`. IF clear.
pub fn kick(f: u64) -> u64 {
    if sv(S_STARTED) == 0 || cur() != IDLE || sv(S_HEAD) == 0 {
        return f
    }
    put_field(IDLE, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    run_next()
}

/// Start the scheduler on the paging structures at `pml4` (the PML4's
/// physical address, from `paging.start`): the caller becomes thread 0,
/// the idle thread gets its stack. Interrupts stay as they are; the
/// caller enables them (`sync.enable`) when it wants preemption.
pub fn start(pml4: u64) {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < SLOTS {
        clear(s)
        s += 1
    }
    put_sv(S_STARTED, 0)
    put_sv(S_PML4, pml4)
    put_sv(S_CURRENT, MAIN)
    put_sv(S_HEAD, 0)
    put_sv(S_TAIL, 0)
    put_sv(S_NEXT_ID, 2)
    put_sv(S_NEXT_PID, 1)
    put_sv(S_LIVE, 0)
    put_sv(S_WAITER, 0)
    put_sv(S_FLOOR, 0)
    put_sv(S_SLICE, timer.ticks())
    put_field(MAIN, offset_of(Thread, state), RUNNING)
    put_field(MAIN, offset_of(Thread, id), 0)
    put_field(MAIN, offset_of(Thread, runs), 1)
    make_stack_or_die(IDLE)
    // px24: sixty-four slots' stacks span two page tables (2 MiB each);
    // the second is taken now, by mapping and unmapping the last slot's
    // stack, so the first process to run more than thirty threads does
    // not leave the count of free frames one short for good.
    make_stack_or_die(SLOTS - 1)
    free_stack(SLOTS - 1)
    put_field(SLOTS - 1, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo), 0)
    put_field(SLOTS - 1, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi), 0)
    fpu.reset(IDLE)
    // # Safety: the address of boot/sched.S's idle loop; nothing is read.
    first_frame(IDLE, unsafe { pax_idle as u64 }, 0, 0)
    put_field(IDLE, offset_of(Thread, id), 1)
    put_field(IDLE, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    put_sv(S_STARTED, 1)
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
}

/// Reap every dead thread but the running one: its stack unmapped, its
/// frames freed, its slot free — or, for a process a parent will wait
/// for (px14), its slot kept as a ZOMBIE holding its pid and status.
/// IF clear, in thread context or a system call (never on the dead
/// thread's own stack: it is not the running one).
fn reap() {
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if s != cur() && field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) == DEAD {
            if field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo)) != 0 { // px26: a held leader's went at its own end
                free_stack(s)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo), 0)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi), 0)
            }
            if field(s, offset_of(Thread, gone)) == 1 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, threads)) > 0 {
                // px26: a leader whose threads live on keeps its slot
                // (the pid, the area, the space) until the last has ended.
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), HELD)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), 0)
                s += 1
                continue
            }
            let c = field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3))
            if c != 0 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, owns)) == 1 {
                let _freed = paging.free_user(c) // px09: the user half, its tables, its PML4
            }
            if field(s, offset_of(Thread, zombie)) == 1 {
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), ZOMBIE)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3), 0)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, owns), 0)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), 0)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo), 0)
                put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi), 0)
            } else {
                clear(s)
            }
        }
        s += 1
    }
}

/// Start a thread running `body` (an `export fn body(arg: u64)`'s
/// address) with `arg`, queued at the tail. Returns its id.
pub fn create(body: u64, arg: u64) -> u64 {
    start_thread(body, arg, 0, 0)
}

/// Start a thread in the address space whose PML4 is at `cr3` (px09):
/// `body(prog)` runs in that space from its first instruction (kernel/
/// user's `pax_user_thread`, which enters ring 3). The space becomes the
/// thread's: `reap` frees it with the stack. Returns its id.
pub fn create_user(body: u64, prog: u64, cr3: u64) -> u64 {
    start_thread(body, prog, cr3, prog + 1)
}

/// Start an ELF process (px10): a user thread in the address space at
/// `cr3`, whose body (kernel/user's `pax_user_thread`, given `arg`)
/// enters ring 3 at `entry` with RSP `usp`, named by the `len` bytes at
/// `name`. Its `prog` is `ELF_PROG`. Returns its id.
pub fn create_process(body: u64, arg: u64, cr3: u64, entry: u64, usp: u64, name: u64, len: u64) -> u64 {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    let id = start_thread(body, arg, cr3, ELF_PROG)
    let s = slot_of(id)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, entry), entry)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, usp), usp)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name), name)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name_len), len)
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
    id
}

fn start_thread(body: u64, arg: u64, cr3: u64, prog: u64) -> u64 {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    reap()
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS && field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) != FREE {
        s += 1
    }
    if s == SLOTS {
        panic.fail("sched: no free thread slot, live", live_count())
    }
    make_stack_or_die(s)
    fpu.reset(s)
    // # Safety: the address of boot/sched.S's entry; nothing is read.
    first_frame(s, unsafe { pax_thread_entry as u64 }, body, arg)
    let id = sv(S_NEXT_ID)
    put_sv(S_NEXT_ID, id + 1)
    put_sv(S_LIVE, sv(S_LIVE) + 1)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, id), id)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3), cr3)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, owns), if cr3 != 0 { 1 } else { 0 })
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, prog), prog)
    if prog != 0 {
        let pid = take_pid()
        put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid), pid) // px14: a process, started by the kernel (ppid 0)
        put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid), pid) // px26: a group of one, its own leader
        put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, threads), 1)
        // px48: it leads a session and a process group of its own, and
        // takes the console as its controlling terminal, its group in the
        // foreground, when the console is a terminal and no session holds
        // it (named: Linux's init is in session 0 with no terminal; this
        // is what a getty or `setsid -c` gives a shell there).
        put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pgid), pid)
        put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, sid), pid)
        if sv(S_TTY_ON) == 1 && sv(S_TTY_SID) == 0 {
            put_sv(S_TTY_SID, pid)
            put_sv(S_TTY_PGRP, pid)
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ctty), 1)
        }
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, lead), s)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    enqueue(s)
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
    id
}

/// The next Linux pid (px14): 1 for the first process, never reused.
fn take_pid() -> u64 {
    let p = sv(S_NEXT_PID)
    put_sv(S_NEXT_PID, p + 1)
    p
}

/// Give the CPU to the next ready thread (back to this one if none).
pub fn yield() {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, yields), field(c, offset_of(Thread, yields)) + 1)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    // # Safety: boot/sched.S's voluntary switch; it returns when this
    // thread is switched in again.
    unsafe { pax_switch() }
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
}

/// Sleep `n` ticks: ready again at the tick `from + n`. Returns `(from,
/// woke)`, both read with IF clear: the tick the sleep began at and the
/// tick this thread ran again at.
pub fn sleep(n: u64) -> (u64, u64) {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    let c = cur()
    let from = timer.ticks()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wake), from + n)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), SLEEPING)
    // # Safety: as `yield`.
    unsafe { pax_switch() }
    let woke = timer.ticks() // #598: a volatile read (kernel/timer)
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
    (from, woke) // #598: `woke` is read after the switch; S2 asserts woke = from + n
}

/// Block until every spawned thread has exited, then reap them.
pub fn wait_all() {
    wait_down_to(0)
}

/// Block until at most `n` spawned threads are live, then reap the dead
/// (px22: a kernel whose own threads never exit — the network's two —
/// waits for the processes it started with `n` the count before them).
pub fn wait_down_to(n: u64) {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    if live_count() > n {
        let c = cur()
        put_sv(S_FLOOR, n)
        put_sv(S_WAITER, c + 1)
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), BLOCKED)
        // # Safety: as `yield`.
        unsafe { pax_switch() }
    }
    reap()
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
}

/// End the running thread. Its stack is reclaimed by the next `create` or
/// `wait_all`. Never returns.
pub fn exit() {
    let _saved = sync.irq_save()
    let c = cur()
    finish(c)
    // # Safety: as `yield`; a dead thread is never switched in again.
    unsafe { pax_switch() }
    panic.fail("sched: a dead thread ran again, slot", c)
}

/// One live thread fewer: the waiter (kmain's `wait_all`) readied when
/// the count has come down to what it waits for. IF clear.
fn thread_gone() {
    let n = sv(S_LIVE) - 1
    put_sv(S_LIVE, n)
    let w = sv(S_WAITER)
    if n <= sv(S_FLOOR) && w != 0 {
        put_field(w - 1, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
        enqueue(w - 1)
        put_sv(S_WAITER, 0)
    }
}

/// The process whose leader is slot `l` has ended with wait status `st`
/// (px14's `finish`, a process's half): a vfork parent it held is
/// resumed; its children go to pid 1 (or, when it is pid 1 or pid 1 is
/// gone, to nobody, and a zombie nobody will wait for is let go); it
/// stays a zombie for a parent, which is woken if it is waiting and
/// (px26) sent SIGCHLD. IF clear.
fn process_ends(l: u64, st: u64) {
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, status), st)
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads), 0)
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend), 0)
    release_vfork(l)
    let me = field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    if me != 0 {
        // px48: a controlling process's end hangs its terminal up: the
        // foreground group is sent SIGHUP (measured: not SIGCONT) and the
        // console belongs to no session (XSH _exit, XBD 11.1.3).
        if field(l, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == me && sv(S_TTY_SID) == me {
            let fg = sv(S_TTY_PGRP)
            tty_leaves(me)
            hangup_group(fg, false)
        }
        // px48: the groups this end may orphan — its own, and each
        // child's — as they stand before it: `kids` has bit t for a child
        // in slot t whose group is not orphaned yet.
        let own = field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid))
        let own_was = own != 0 && !orphaned_but(own, SLOTS)
        var kids: u64 = 0
        var t: u64 = FIRST
        while t < SLOTS {
            if t != l && live_leader(t) && field(t, offset_of(Thread, ppid)) == me && !orphaned_but(field(t, offset_of(Thread, pgid)), SLOTS) {
                kids = kids | (1 << t)
            }
            t += 1
        }
        reparent(l, me)
        // A group this end orphaned that has a stopped member is sent
        // SIGHUP, then SIGCONT (XSH _exit; measured), once.
        if own_was && orphaned_but(own, l) && has_stopped(own) {
            hangup_group(own, true)
        }
        t = FIRST
        while t < SLOTS {
            if kids & (1 << t) != 0 {
                let g = field(t, offset_of(Thread, pgid))
                var first = g != own || !own_was
                var u: u64 = FIRST
                while u < t {
                    if kids & (1 << u) != 0 && field(u, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == g {
                        first = false
                    }
                    u += 1
                }
                if first && orphaned_but(g, l) && has_stopped(g) {
                    hangup_group(g, true)
                }
            }
            t += 1
        }
        let pp = field(l, offset_of(Thread, ppid))
        let ps = if pp != 0 { live_slot(pp) } else { SLOTS }
        if ps != SLOTS {
            put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, zombie), 1)
            wake_waiter(pp)
            // px26: SIGCHLD for the parent (`signal(7)`; siginfo's
            // CLD_EXITED with the exit code, CLD_KILLED with the signal).
            let killed = st & 0x7f != 0
            // px45: and the child's real user id, si_uid (measured).
            raise_group(ps, SIGCHLD, sig_info(if killed { I_CHLD_KILLED } else { I_CHLD_EXITED }, me, if killed { st & 0x7f } else { (st >> 8) & 0xff }, real_uid(l)))
        }
    }
}

/// Mark slot `c`, a kernel thread or the one thread of its process, dead
/// (px14's `finish`): one live thread fewer, and its process's end.
/// IF clear.
fn finish(c: u64) {
    thread_gone()
    process_ends(c, field(c, offset_of(Thread, status)))
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), DEAD)
}

/// Whether slot `s` holds a process that has not ended (px26: a leader
/// held for its threads has not).
fn running_state(s: u64) -> bool {
    let st = field(s, offset_of(Thread, state))
    st != FREE && st != DEAD && st != ZOMBIE
}

/// The leader's slot of the process `pid` that has not ended; SLOTS if
/// none.
fn live_slot(pid: u64) -> u64 {
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid)) == pid && field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == pid && running_state(s) {
            return s
        }
        s += 1
    }
    SLOTS
}

/// The process `pid`: every thread of it in wait4/waitid made ready
/// (px26: any thread of a parent may be the one waiting); its call runs
/// again and finds what changed.
fn wake_waiter(pid: u64) {
    var p: u64 = FIRST
    while p < SLOTS {
        if field(p, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == pid && field(p, offset_of(Thread, state)) == WAITING {
            put_field(p, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
            enqueue(p)
        }
        p += 1
    }
}

/// px26: slot `s` taken out of the run queue, if it is in it (a thread
/// ended by another's `exit_group` while it was ready). IF clear.
fn unqueue(s: u64) {
    var prev: u64 = 0
    var h = sv(S_HEAD)
    while h != 0 {
        let n = field(h - 1, offset_of(Thread, next))
        if h - 1 == s {
            if prev == 0 {
                put_sv(S_HEAD, n)
            } else {
                put_field(prev - 1, offset_of(Thread, next), n)
            }
            if n == 0 {
                put_sv(S_TAIL, prev)
            }
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, next), 0)
            return
        }
        prev = h
        h = n
    }
}

/// px26: every thread of the group led by slot `l` but slot `keep` ended
/// at once, wherever it was waiting or queued (`exit_group`, a fatal
/// signal, `execve`): none of them is running (one CPU, and `keep` is),
/// so each is simply dead, its stack reaped later like any other. A
/// leader already held stays held: the caller decides its slot's fate.
/// IF clear.
fn end_others(l: u64, keep: u64) {
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        let st = field(t, offset_of(Thread, state))
        if t != keep && st != FREE && st != DEAD && st != ZOMBIE && st != HELD && field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)) == l && field(t, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) != 0 {
            if st == READY {
                unqueue(t)
            }
            // A vfork child one of them was waiting for keeps running in
            // the space it borrowed: nothing is done to it here, and
            // `own_borrowed` (below) leaves the space to it.
            thread_gone()
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, state), DEAD)
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE)
        }
        t += 1
    }
}

/// px26: the group led by `l` is ending while a vfork child still runs
/// in its address space (a thread of it was suspended for that child,
/// and another thread ended the process): the space becomes the
/// child's, which frees it when it execs or ends; the group's slots no
/// longer own it. Returns whether a child took it. IF clear.
fn own_borrowed(l: u64) -> bool {
    let c = field(l, offset_of(Thread, cr3))
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        let vp = field(t, offset_of(Thread, vparent))
        if vp != 0 && field(t, offset_of(Thread, state)) != FREE && field(t, offset_of(Thread, cr3)) == c && field(t, offset_of(Thread, owns)) == 0 && field(vp - 1, offset_of(Thread, lead)) == l && field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)) != l {
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, owns), 1)
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, vparent), 0)
            put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, owns), 0)
            return true
        }
        t += 1
    }
    false
}

/// Slot `c`'s vfork parent, if it holds one suspended, resumed.
fn release_vfork(c: u64) {
    let vp = field(c, offset_of(Thread, vparent))
    if vp == 0 {
        return
    }
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, vparent), 0)
    if field(vp - 1, offset_of(Thread, state)) == VFORK {
        put_field(vp - 1, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
        enqueue(vp - 1)
    }
}

/// The children of `me` (slot `c`, ending) given to pid 1, or to
/// nobody when `me` is 1 or pid 1 has ended: a zombie nobody will
/// wait for is let go at once, a dead one at its reap.
fn reparent(c: u64, me: u64) {
    let heir = if me != 1 && live_slot(1) != SLOTS { 1 } else { 0 }
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        let st = field(t, offset_of(Thread, state))
        if t != c && st != FREE && field(t, offset_of(Thread, ppid)) == me {
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, ppid), heir)
            if heir == 0 {
                if st == ZOMBIE {
                    clear(t)
                } else {
                    put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, zombie), 0)
                }
            } else if st == ZOMBIE || st == DEAD {
                wake_waiter(heir)
            }
        }
        t += 1
    }
}

/// End the running thread from a handler (px09: a system call's `exit`,
/// a fault in ring 3), where `exit`'s `pax_switch` cannot be used: the
/// thread is dead at once, its frame `_f` is dropped, and the frame
/// returned is the next thread's, for the handler to resume. Its stack
/// and address space are reaped later, in thread context.
pub fn end_current(_f: u64) -> u64 {
    end_with(_f, 0)
}

/// `end_current` with the wait status word `st` kept for the parent
/// (px14: `code << 8` for an exit, the signal's number for a kill).
/// px26: this ends the PROCESS — every thread of the caller's group
/// (`exit_group`, a fatal signal, a fault, the last thread's `exit`).
pub fn end_with(_f: u64, st: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    let l = field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    speaker.ended(field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid))) // px18: its tone stops with it
    end_others(l, c)
    let _taken = own_borrowed(l)
    process_ends(l, st)
    if l != c {
        // The leader is another slot: ended above, or held since its own
        // `exit`. Either way it is dead now, and its reap gives the
        // space back and keeps the zombie.
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, gone), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, state), DEAD)
    }
    thread_gone()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), DEAD)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE)
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px26: `exit` (60) from a thread whose group lives on: this thread
/// alone ends. Its frame is dropped; a leader's slot is kept (`gone`)
/// for the group, any other's is free at the next reap. The caller
/// (kernel/process) has cleared and woken the clear-child-tid word.
/// With no other thread left the caller uses `end_with` instead. IF
/// clear. The next thread's frame.
pub fn end_thread(_f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    let l = field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads), field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads)) - 1)
    if c == l {
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, gone), 1)
    }
    thread_gone()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), DEAD)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE)
    poke(l) // a process's pending signal may now be another thread's to take
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px26: how many threads of the running thread's process have not
/// ended.
pub fn self_threads() -> u64 {
    field(field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead)), offset_of(Thread, threads))
}

/// boot/sched.S's pax_thread_entry calls this when a body returns.
export fn pax_thread_exit() {
    exit()
}

/// The running thread's id.
pub fn self_id() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, id))
}

/// How many times the running thread has been switched in (1 on its
/// first run).
pub fn self_runs() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, runs))
}

/// The running thread's program (kernel/user's number + 1; 0 for a
/// kernel thread, px09).
pub fn self_prog() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, prog))
}

/// The running thread's entry point and initial user stack pointer
/// (`which` 0 and 1), and its name's first byte and length (2 and 3):
/// an ELF process's (px10), 0 for any other thread.
pub fn self_exec(which: int) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    if which == 0 {
        return field(c, offset_of(Thread, entry))
    }
    if which == 1 {
        return field(c, offset_of(Thread, usp))
    }
    if which == 2 {
        return field(c, offset_of(Thread, name))
    }
    field(c, offset_of(Thread, name_len))
}

/// How many times the running thread has called `yield`.
pub fn self_yields() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, yields))
}

/// How many times the idle thread has been switched in.
pub fn idle_runs() -> u64 {
    field(IDLE, offset_of(Thread, runs))
}

/// Spawned threads not yet exited.
pub fn live_count() -> u64 {
    sv(S_LIVE)
}

/// The slot of the live thread with id `id`; SLOTS if none.
fn slot_of(id: u64) -> u64 {
    var s: u64 = IDLE
    while s < SLOTS {
        if field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) != FREE && field(s, offset_of(Thread, id)) == id {
            return s
        }
        s += 1
    }
    SLOTS
}

/// Thread `id`'s lowest stack byte (0 if no such thread).
pub fn stack_lo(id: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = slot_of(id)
    if s == SLOTS {
        return 0
    }
    field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_lo))
}

/// Thread `id`'s first byte past its stack (0 if no such thread).
pub fn stack_hi(id: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = slot_of(id)
    if s == SLOTS {
        return 0
    }
    field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi))
}

/// Thread `id`'s guard's lowest byte (0 if no such thread); the guard
/// ends at `stack_lo(id)`.
pub fn guard_lo(id: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = slot_of(id)
    if s == SLOTS {
        return 0
    }
    base_of(s)
}

/// The id of the thread whose guard holds `a`, plus one; 0 when `a` is
/// in no live thread's guard (kernel/interrupts' fatal path).
pub fn guard_of(a: u64) -> u64 {
    if sv(S_STARTED) == 0 || a < STACKS || a >= STACKS + SLOTS * SPAN {
        return 0
    }
    let s = (a - STACKS) / SPAN
    if s == MAIN || field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) == FREE {
        return 0
    }
    if a - base_of(s) >= GUARD {
        return 0
    }
    field(s, offset_of(Thread, id)) + 1
}

/// The PML4 the stacks are mapped in.
pub fn pml4() -> u64 {
    sv(S_PML4)
}

// --- processes (px14) --------------------------------------------------------

/// The running process's pid (0 for a kernel thread). px26: the
/// process's, whichever of its threads is running (`getpid`).
pub fn self_pid() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, tgid))
}

/// px26: the running thread's own id (`gettid`; the leader's is the pid).
pub fn self_tid() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, pid))
}

/// The running process's parent's pid (0: started by the kernel).
pub fn self_ppid() -> u64 {
    field(field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead)), offset_of(Thread, ppid))
}

/// Thread `id`'s pid (0 if no such thread or not a process): the
/// number `who` lines print.
pub fn pid_of(id: u64) -> u64 {
    let s = slot_of(id)
    if s == SLOTS {
        return 0
    }
    field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid))
}

/// The running process's blocked signals.
pub fn self_sigmask() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, sigmask))
}

pub fn set_self_sigmask(v: u64) {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, sigmask), v)
}

/// A child of the running process made from its system call frame `f`
/// (`clone`/`clone3`/`vfork`): a new slot and kernel stack, a new pid,
/// the caller its parent; its first frame is `f`'s 22 words with %rax 0
/// and, when `usp` is not 0, %rsp `usp`, so it resumes in ring 3 after
/// the `syscall` as the caller would; the caller's FS base, signal mask,
/// x87/SSE state and process area (descriptors, break, signal records)
/// copied. Its space: `cr3` 0, the caller's, borrowed and not owned
/// (CLONE_VM, px14); otherwise `cr3`, a copy made for it (a fork, px17),
/// its own. Queued ready. Returns its slot; SLOTS when no slot is free
/// (nothing made, `cr3` not taken). IF clear (a system call).
pub fn clone_current(f: u64, usp: u64, cr3: u64) -> u64 {
    let p = cur()
    reap()
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS && field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) != FREE {
        s += 1
    }
    if s == SLOTS {
        return SLOTS
    }
    if !make_stack(s) {
        return NO_STACK // px34
    }
    fpu.fork(p, s)
    let hi = field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi))
    let cf = hi - FRAME_WORDS * 8
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < FRAME_WORDS {
        // # Safety: [cf, hi) is inside the stack just mapped; [f, f + 176)
        // is the caller's live frame on its own kernel stack.
        unsafe { ((cf + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(((f + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile()) }
        i += 1
    }
    // # Safety: as above.
    unsafe {
        ((cf + W_RAX * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0)
        if usp != 0 {
            ((cf + W_RSP * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(usp)
        }
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), cf)
    let id = sv(S_NEXT_ID)
    put_sv(S_NEXT_ID, id + 1)
    put_sv(S_LIVE, sv(S_LIVE) + 1)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, id), id)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3), if cr3 == 0 { field(p, offset_of(Thread, cr3)) } else { cr3 })
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, owns), if cr3 == 0 { 0 } else { 1 })
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, prog), field(p, offset_of(Thread, prog)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, entry), field(p, offset_of(Thread, entry)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, usp), field(p, offset_of(Thread, usp)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name_len), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name_len)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, fs), field(p, offset_of(Thread, fs)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, sigmask), field(p, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)))
    // px24: a fork's child keeps the caller's alternate signal stack (sigaltstack(2)).
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ss_sp), field(p, offset_of(Thread, ss_sp)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ss_size), field(p, offset_of(Thread, ss_size)))
    let pid = take_pid()
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid), pid)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid), pid) // px26: a new process, a group of one
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, lead), s)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, threads), 1)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name0), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name0)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name1), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name1)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ppid), field(p, offset_of(Thread, tgid))) // px26: the caller's process
    // px48: a fork's child is in its parent's group and session, with its
    // controlling terminal; it has not exec'd, and it runs.
    let pl = field(p, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pgid), field(pl, offset_of(Thread, pgid)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, sid), field(pl, offset_of(Thread, sid)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ctty), field(pl, offset_of(Thread, ctty)))
    let from = proc_area(p) // px26: the caller's process's area, whichever thread calls
    let to = own_area(s)
    i = 0
    while i < PROC_AREA / 8 {
        // # Safety: two slots' areas inside boot/sched.S's pax_procs.
        unsafe { ((to + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(((from + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile()) }
        i += 1
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    enqueue(s)
    s
}

/// px26: a THREAD of the running thread's process made from its system
/// call frame `f` (`clone`/`clone3` with CLONE_THREAD): a new slot and
/// kernel stack and a tid of its own, in the caller's group — the same
/// leader, so the same area (descriptors, working directory, break,
/// signal actions), the same address space (not its own to free) and
/// the same pid. Its first frame is `f`'s with %rax 0 and %rsp `usp`
/// (a thread always names its stack); its FS base `tls` when `settls`,
/// else the caller's; the caller's signal mask and x87/SSE state; its
/// clear-child-tid word `ctid` (0 none). Queued ready. Returns its slot;
/// SLOTS when no slot is free. IF clear (a system call).
pub fn clone_thread(f: u64, usp: u64, settls: bool, tls: u64, ctid: u64) -> u64 {
    let p = cur()
    let l = field(p, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    reap()
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS && field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) != FREE {
        s += 1
    }
    if s == SLOTS {
        return SLOTS
    }
    if !make_stack(s) {
        return NO_STACK // px34
    }
    fpu.fork(p, s)
    let hi = field(s, offset_of(Thread, stack_hi))
    let cf = hi - FRAME_WORDS * 8
    var i: u64 = 0
    while i < FRAME_WORDS {
        // # Safety: as `clone_current`.
        unsafe { ((cf + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(((f + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile()) }
        i += 1
    }
    // # Safety: as above.
    unsafe {
        ((cf + W_RAX * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0)
        if usp != 0 {
            ((cf + W_RSP * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(usp)
        }
    }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, frame), cf)
    let id = sv(S_NEXT_ID)
    put_sv(S_NEXT_ID, id + 1)
    put_sv(S_LIVE, sv(S_LIVE) + 1)
    put_sv(S_THREADS, sv(S_THREADS) + 1)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, id), id)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3), field(l, offset_of(Thread, cr3)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, owns), 0)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, prog), field(p, offset_of(Thread, prog)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, entry), field(p, offset_of(Thread, entry)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, usp), field(p, offset_of(Thread, usp)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name_len), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name_len)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, fs), if settls { tls } else { field(p, offset_of(Thread, fs)) })
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, sigmask), field(p, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid), take_pid())
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid), field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, lead), l)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ppid), 0) // a thread is nobody's child: wait4 sees processes
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, ctid), ctid)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name0), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name0)))
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, name1), field(p, offset_of(Thread, name1)))
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads), field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads)) + 1)
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    enqueue(s)
    s
}

/// px26: threads made by `clone` since boot (kernel/process's census
/// line every 2500th).
pub fn threads_made() -> u64 {
    sv(S_THREADS)
}

/// px26: slots that are not free (the same census line).
pub fn slots_busy() -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = 0
    while s < SLOTS {
        if field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) != FREE {
            n += 1
        }
        s += 1
    }
    n
}

/// px26: before `execve`'s point of no return in a process with more
/// than one thread: every other thread ends, and the caller becomes the
/// leader if it was not — the pid, the parent, the area (copied into its
/// own slot), the address space and the process's pending signals are
/// its own from here on, and its tid is the pid (`execve(2)`: "all
/// threads other than the calling thread are destroyed … the thread ID
/// of the new program is the process ID"). The old leader's slot, held
/// or just ended, is let go without the space. IF clear.
pub fn exec_alone() {
    let c = cur()
    let l = field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    if field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads)) <= 1 && l == c {
        return
    }
    end_others(l, c)
    if l != c {
        let from = own_area(l)
        let to = own_area(c)
        var i: u64 = 0
        while i < PROC_AREA / 8 {
            // # Safety: two slots' areas inside boot/sched.S's pax_procs.
            unsafe { ((to + i * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(((from + i * 8) as *u64).read_volatile()) }
            i += 1
        }
        i = 0
        while i < NSIG {
            put_info(c, 1, i + 1, info_of(l, 1, i + 1))
            i += 1
        }
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, pid), field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ppid), field(l, offset_of(Thread, ppid)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, owns), field(l, offset_of(Thread, owns)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, vparent), field(l, offset_of(Thread, vparent)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, gpend), field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend)))
        // px48: the process's group, session, terminal and reports.
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, pgid), field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, sid), field(l, offset_of(Thread, sid)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ctty), field(l, offset_of(Thread, ctty)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, execd), field(l, offset_of(Thread, execd)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, stopped), field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wstop), field(l, offset_of(Thread, wstop)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wcont), field(l, offset_of(Thread, wcont)))
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead), c)
        // The old leader: no pid, no parent, no space; its stack (if it
        // still has one) and its slot go at the next reap.
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, pid), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, ppid), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, cr3), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, owns), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, vparent), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, zombie), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, gone), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, threads), 0)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, lead), l)
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, state), DEAD)
    }
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, threads), 1)
}

/// Slot `s`'s pid (px14).
pub fn slot_pid(s: u64) -> u64 {
    field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid))
}

// --- px25: processes by pid, for /proc/<pid> (kernel/procfs) ------------------

/// Whether slot `s` holds a process's leader that has not been reaped:
/// a pid, its own group's leader, not free or dead (a zombie and a held
/// leader count, as Linux keeps `/proc/<pid>` until the reap). px45: a
/// process that has ended for a parent to wait for is a zombie from its
/// end, not from the next `reap` (which runs at the next fork, clone or
/// wait): before, it was no process at all in between — `/proc/<pid>`
/// -ENOENT and `kill` -ESRCH — where Linux's is a zombie at once.
fn is_proc(s: u64) -> bool {
    let st = field(s, offset_of(Thread, state))
    let p = field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid))
    st != FREE && (st != DEAD || ended(s)) && p != 0 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == p
}

/// px45: whether slot `s` is a process that has ended and waits for its
/// parent: a ZOMBIE, or DEAD and marked for one (`process_ends`) with no
/// thread left, before `reap` makes it a ZOMBIE.
fn ended(s: u64) -> bool {
    let st = field(s, offset_of(Thread, state))
    st == ZOMBIE || (st == DEAD && field(s, offset_of(Thread, zombie)) == 1 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, threads)) == 0)
}

/// px25: the slot of process `pid`'s leader, SLOTS if no such process.
pub fn proc_slot(pid: u64) -> u64 {
    if pid == 0 {
        return SLOTS
    }
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if is_proc(s) && field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid)) == pid {
            return s
        }
        s += 1
    }
    SLOTS
}

/// px25: the live processes' pids in increasing order: the smallest
/// pid above `after`, 0 when there is none.
pub fn proc_after(after: u64) -> u64 {
    var best: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if is_proc(s) {
            let p = field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid))
            if p > after && (best == 0 || p < best) {
                best = p
            }
        }
        s += 1
    }
    best
}

/// px25: what /proc/<pid>/status reads of the leader in slot `s`:
/// 0 its parent's pid, 1 its threads not ended, 2 its blocked signals,
/// 3 its PML4 (0 for a zombie: its space is given back), 4 and 5 its
/// PR_SET_NAME words, 6 its program name's first byte, 7 that name's
/// length, 8 1 when it is a zombie; px48: 9 its process group, 10 its
/// session, 11 1 when it is stopped, 12 1 when the console is its
/// controlling terminal, 13 its signals pending (its own thread's and
/// the process's).
pub fn proc_word(s: u64, which: int) -> u64 {
    if which == 9 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, pgid))
    }
    if which == 10 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, sid))
    }
    if which == 11 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, stopped))
    }
    if which == 12 {
        return if has_tty(s) { 1 } else { 0 }
    }
    if which == 13 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, pend)) | field(s, offset_of(Thread, gpend))
    }
    let zombie = ended(s) // px45: from its end (`is_proc`)
    if which == 0 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, ppid))
    }
    if which == 1 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, threads))
    }
    if which == 2 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, sigmask))
    }
    if which == 3 {
        return if zombie { 0 } else { field(s, offset_of(Thread, cr3)) }
    }
    if which == 4 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, name0))
    }
    if which == 5 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, name1))
    }
    if which == 6 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, name))
    }
    if which == 7 {
        return field(s, offset_of(Thread, name_len))
    }
    if zombie { 1 } else { 0 }
}

/// px25: the process area of the leader in slot `s` (its own).
pub fn proc_area_of_leader(s: u64) -> u64 {
    own_area(s)
}

/// Suspend the running process until its vfork child in slot `s` execs
/// or ends (`release_vfork`): its frame `f` saved with %rax the child's
/// pid, the frame returned the next thread's. IF clear.
pub fn vfork_wait(f: u64, s: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    // # Safety: the caller's live frame (22 words).
    unsafe { ((f + W_RAX * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid))) }
    put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, vparent), c + 1)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), VFORK)
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// The running process takes the address space at `cr3` (px14,
/// `execve`): made live, its own; the old one given back when it was
/// its own, handed back to the vfork parent (resumed) when borrowed.
/// The entry, the initial stack pointer and the name are the new
/// program's; the FS base 0. IF clear (a system call).
pub fn exec_current(cr3: u64, entry: u64, usp: u64, name: u64, len: u64) {
    let c = cur()
    let old = field(c, offset_of(Thread, cr3))
    let owned = field(c, offset_of(Thread, owns))
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, cr3), cr3)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, owns), 1)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, prog), ELF_PROG)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, entry), entry)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, usp), usp)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, name), name)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, name_len), len)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, fs), 0)
    // px26: the new program starts with no clear-child-tid word and
    // the program's own name; pending signals stay.
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ctid), 0)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ss_sp), 0) // px24: execve drops the alternate signal stack
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ss_size), 0)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, name0), 0)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, name1), 0)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, execd), 1) // px48: setpgid of it by its parent is -EACCES from here
    paging.load(cr3)
    // # Safety: as in `enter_space`.
    unsafe { pax_wrmsr(FS_BASE, 0) }
    if old != 0 && owned == 1 {
        let _freed = paging.free_user(old)
    }
    release_vfork(c)
}

/// What the running process may wait for (px14, wait4/waitid): `sel`
/// 0 any child, else the child with that pid. Dead threads are reaped
/// first. Returns (whether it has such a child at all, the slot of one
/// that has ended or SLOTS).
pub fn child_ended(sel: u64) -> (bool, u64) {
    let (any, s, _w) = wait_find(if sel == 0 { 0 } else { 1 }, sel, W_EXITED)
    (any, s)
}

/// px48: what a wait selects and wants (wait4(2), waitid(2)): `how` 0
/// any child, 1 the child whose pid is `sel`, 2 the children in process
/// group `sel`; `want` W_EXITED, W_STOPPED, W_CONTINUED. Returns
/// (whether such a child exists at all, the slot of the first in slot
/// order with something to report or SLOTS, what: W_EXITED a zombie,
/// W_STOPPED a stop not yet reported, W_CONTINUED a continue not yet
/// reported). Dead threads are reaped first.
pub const W_EXITED: u64 = 1
pub const W_STOPPED: u64 = 2
pub const W_CONTINUED: u64 = 4

pub fn wait_find(how: u64, sel: u64, want: u64) -> (bool, u64, u64) {
    reap()
    let me = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, tgid)) // px26: the caller's process
    var any = false
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        let st = field(s, offset_of(Thread, state))
        let ok = how == 0 || (how == 1 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid)) == sel) || (how == 2 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == sel)
        if st != FREE && field(s, offset_of(Thread, ppid)) == me && field(s, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid)) && ok {
            any = true
            if st == ZOMBIE {
                if want & W_EXITED != 0 {
                    return (true, s, W_EXITED)
                }
            } else if running_state(s) {
                if want & W_STOPPED != 0 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, wstop)) != 0 {
                    return (true, s, W_STOPPED)
                }
                if want & W_CONTINUED != 0 && field(s, offset_of(Thread, wcont)) != 0 {
                    return (true, s, W_CONTINUED)
                }
            }
        }
        s += 1
    }
    (any, SLOTS, 0)
}

/// px48: a stop or continue report of the child in slot `s`: (its pid,
/// the stop's signal, or 0 for a continue); `consume` clears it (a wait
/// without WNOWAIT).
pub fn wait_report(s: u64, what: u64, consume: bool) -> (u64, u64) {
    let pid = field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid))
    if what == W_STOPPED {
        let sig = field(s, offset_of(Thread, wstop))
        if consume {
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, wstop), 0)
        }
        return (pid, sig)
    }
    if consume {
        put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, wcont), 0)
    }
    (pid, 0)
}

/// The zombie in slot `s`: (its pid, its status word); `reap_it` lets the
/// slot go (wait4, waitid without WNOWAIT).
pub fn zombie(s: u64, reap_it: bool) -> (u64, u64) {
    let r = (field(s, offset_of(Thread, pid)), field(s, offset_of(Thread, status)))
    if reap_it {
        clear(s)
    }
    r
}

/// Block the running process in a read or write of a pipe or socket
/// (px17): its frame `f`, rewound by kernel/user to run the call again,
/// saved; the thread waits until `wake_pipes`; the next thread's frame.
/// IF clear.
pub fn block_pipe(f: u64) -> u64 {
    block_pipe_until(f, 0)
}

/// `block_pipe` with a deadline (px22: a socket's receive timeout): the
/// thread is made ready by `wake_pipes` or at tick `wake`, whichever
/// comes first, and runs its call again either way; `wake` 0 is no
/// deadline. IF clear.
pub fn block_pipe_until(f: u64, wake: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wake), wake)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), PIPE)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), if wake == 0 { W_REWOUND } else { W_TIMED_REWOUND }) // px26
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px24: `block_pipe_until` for a call a signal never restarts (`poll`,
/// `epoll_wait`: `signal(7)`): with or without a deadline, a handled
/// signal ends it with -EINTR. IF clear.
pub fn block_pipe_eintr(f: u64, wake: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wake), wake)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), PIPE)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_TIMED_REWOUND)
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px24: the tick the running thread's waiting call gives up at; 0
/// when it has no deadline. The thread's own word: two threads of one
/// process may each wait with a timeout (px22 kept a receive's in the
/// process's area).
pub fn self_until() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, until))
}

pub fn set_self_until(v: u64) {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, until), v)
}

/// px24: the running thread's alternate signal stack (`sigaltstack`):
/// its lowest byte and its size; (0, 0) when it has none.
pub fn self_altstack() -> (u64, u64) {
    let c = cur()
    (field(c, offset_of(Thread, ss_sp)), field(c, offset_of(Thread, ss_size)))
}

pub fn set_self_altstack(sp: u64, size: u64) {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ss_sp), sp)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ss_size), size)
}

/// Make every thread waiting on a pipe or socket ready (px17: some pipe
/// changed; each runs its call again). IF clear.
pub fn wake_pipes() {
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) == PIPE {
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
            enqueue(s)
        }
        s += 1
    }
}

/// Block the running kernel thread until `wake_net` (px20: the network
/// thread, when the device has nothing for it). The caller has IF
/// clear and has just seen nothing pending, so the device's interrupt
/// cannot fall between its look and this wait. Returns when woken.
pub fn block_net() {
    let saved = sync.irq_save()
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, state), NET)
    // # Safety: as `yield`.
    unsafe { pax_switch() }
    sync.irq_restore(saved)
}

/// Make every thread waiting for the network device ready (px20: from
/// its interrupt). Returns how many. IF clear.
pub fn wake_net() -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) == NET {
            put_field(s, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
            enqueue(s)
            n += 1
        }
        s += 1
    }
    n
}

/// Block the running process in wait4/waitid until a child ends (its
/// frame `f` already rewound by the caller to run the call again); the
/// next thread's frame. IF clear.
pub fn wait_child(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), WAITING)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_REWOUND) // px26
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

// --- threads' words (px26) ---------------------------------------------------

/// The running thread's clear-child-tid address (0 none).
pub fn self_ctid() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, ctid))
}

/// `set_tid_address`: the running thread's clear-child-tid address.
pub fn set_self_ctid(v: u64) {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, ctid), v)
}

/// The running thread's name (`which` 0 and 1: PR_SET_NAME's sixteen
/// bytes as two words; both 0 when it never set one).
pub fn self_name(which: int) -> u64 {
    if which == 0 {
        return field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, name0))
    }
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, name1))
}

pub fn set_self_name(a: u64, b: u64) {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, name0), a)
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, name1), b)
}

/// Bytes a waiting pipe write of the running thread has written so far
/// (kernel/files; a word of the area before a process had threads).
pub fn self_done() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, done))
}

pub fn set_self_done(v: u64) {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, done), v)
}

/// A system call begins (kernel/user): whatever wait the thread's last
/// call was in is over.
pub fn syscall_begins() {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE)
}

/// How the running thread's system call was waiting when it was made
/// ready (W_*), and that wait's argument; read once by kernel/signal
/// when it delivers a signal, and cleared.
pub fn self_wait() -> (u64, u64) {
    let c = cur()
    let r = (field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst)), field(c, offset_of(Thread, rarg)))
    if field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst)) == W_TIMED_REWOUND {
        put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, until), 0) // px24: the call will not run again: its deadline goes with it
    }
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rarg), 0)
    r
}

/// The tick the running thread's last timed wait was to end at (a sleep
/// a signal cut short: kernel/signal writes the time left).
pub fn self_wake() -> u64 {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, wake))
}

/// `sched_yield` from a system call: the running thread goes to the
/// queue's tail with its frame `f` (the result already in it); the next
/// thread's frame. IF clear.
pub fn yield_current(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    if sv(S_HEAD) == 0 {
        return f
    }
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
    enqueue(c)
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

// --- futexes (px26) ----------------------------------------------------------
//
// A futex has no entry anywhere: a waiter is a thread in state FUTEX
// whose slot names the word it waits on (its physical address: threads
// of one process meet on it, and PAX shares no frame between address
// spaces), the bitset it waits with and its place in line. A wake walks
// the sixteen slots. A system call runs with IF clear from entry to the
// switch, so no waker runs between the caller's look at the word
// (kernel/process) and this wait.

/// Put the running thread to wait on the futex `key` with bitset `bits`
/// until a wake, tick `wake` (0: no deadline) or a signal: its frame `f`
/// saved with -ETIMEDOUT as the result a deadline leaves (a wake writes
/// 0, kernel/signal -EINTR or the call again); the next thread's frame.
/// IF clear.
pub fn futex_wait(f: u64, key: u64, bits: u64, wake: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    // # Safety: the caller's live frame (22 words).
    unsafe { ((f + W_RAX_OFF) as *u64).write_volatile(NEG_ETIMEDOUT) }
    let q = sv(S_FSEQ) + 1
    put_sv(S_FSEQ, q)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, fkey), key)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, fbits), bits)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, fseq), q)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, wake), wake)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), FUTEX)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), if wake == 0 { W_FUTEX } else { W_TIMED })
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// Wake at most `n` threads waiting on the futex `key` whose bitset
/// shares a bit with `bits`, the longest-waiting first: each is made
/// ready with 0 as its call's result. Returns how many. IF clear.
pub fn futex_wake(key: u64, bits: u64, n: u64) -> u64 {
    var woke: u64 = 0
    while woke < n {
        var best: u64 = SLOTS
        var s: u64 = FIRST
        while s < SLOTS {
            if field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) == FUTEX && field(s, offset_of(Thread, fkey)) == key && field(s, offset_of(Thread, fbits)) & bits != 0 {
                if best == SLOTS || field(s, offset_of(Thread, fseq)) < field(best, offset_of(Thread, fseq)) {
                    best = s
                }
            }
            s += 1
        }
        if best == SLOTS {
            break
        }
        let bf = field(best, offset_of(Thread, frame))
        // # Safety: a waiting thread's saved frame, on its own kernel stack.
        unsafe { ((bf + W_RAX_OFF) as *u64).write_volatile(0) }
        put_field(best, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_NONE)
        put_field(best, offset_of(Thread, fkey), 0)
        put_field(best, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
        enqueue(best)
        woke += 1
    }
    woke
}

/// How many threads wait on a futex now (the soak's census line).
pub fn futex_waiters() -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var s: u64 = FIRST
    while s < SLOTS {
        if field(s, offset_of(Thread, state)) == FUTEX {
            n += 1
        }
        s += 1
    }
    n
}

// --- signals: what is pending, and who is woken (px26) ----------------------------
//
// A thread's slot holds the signals pending for it (`pend`), and its
// leader's the signals pending for the process (`gpend`); one word of
// boot/sched.S's `pax_siginfo` per signal and kind says where each came
// from (`sig_info`). kernel/signal decides what a taken signal does;
// this module only keeps the sets, picks the thread, and makes a
// waiting thread ready for a signal that will run a handler or end its
// process (`poke`).

/// What a pending signal's word says of its origin, for siginfo
/// (kernel/signal): a `kill` (SI_USER), a `tgkill` or `tkill`
/// (SI_TKILL), the terminal (SI_KERNEL), a child's end (CLD_EXITED,
/// CLD_KILLED), a write to a pipe with no reader (SI_USER from itself).
pub const I_USER: u64 = 1
pub const I_TKILL: u64 = 2
pub const I_KERNEL: u64 = 3
pub const I_CHLD_EXITED: u64 = 4
pub const I_CHLD_KILLED: u64 = 5
pub const I_PIPE: u64 = 6
pub const I_CONN: u64 = 7 // px23's: a write to a stream socket whose connection is over
pub const I_CHLD_STOPPED: u64 = 8 // px48: a child stopped (CLD_STOPPED, the signal)
pub const I_CHLD_CONTINUED: u64 = 9 // px48: a child continued (CLD_CONTINUED, SIGCONT)

/// An origin word: the kind (four bits; three before px48), a status (a
/// child's exit code or signal: eight bits), the sender's (or the
/// child's) pid (twenty bits since px48, twenty-one before: a pid above
/// 1,048,575 is carried modulo that, named), and px45: the sender's (or
/// the child's) real user id, all 32 bits — siginfo's `si_uid`
/// (measured on Linux: the real id, not the effective one).
pub fn sig_info(kind: u64, pid: u64, status: u64, uid: u64) -> u64 {
    (kind & 15) | ((status & 0xff) << 4) | ((pid & 0xfffff) << 12) | ((uid & 0xffffffff) << 32)
}

/// px45: the kind, pid, status and user id an origin word carries
/// (kernel/signal's siginfo).
pub fn info_parts(info: u64) -> (u64, u64, u64, u64) {
    (info & 15, (info >> 12) & 0xfffff, (info >> 4) & 0xff, info >> 32)
}

/// px45: the real user id of the process whose leader is slot `l`:
/// kernel/cred's record, word 160 of the process area (kernel/cred's
/// C_UID; read here by its place, since kernel/cred imports what imports
/// this module and wolf refuses an import cycle).
fn real_uid(l: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: the leader's own area, word 160 < 512.
    unsafe { ((own_area(l) + 160 * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn info_of(s: u64, group: u64, sig: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: s < SLOTS, group < 2, 1 <= sig <= 64: inside the 16 * 2 *
    // 64 words of boot/sched.S's pax_siginfo.
    unsafe { ((pax_siginfo as u64 + ((s * 2 + group) * NSIG + sig - 1) * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

fn put_info(s: u64, group: u64, sig: u64, v: u64) {
    // # Safety: as `info_of`.
    unsafe { ((pax_siginfo as u64 + ((s * 2 + group) * NSIG + sig - 1) * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(v) }
}

fn bit(sig: u64) -> u64 {
    1 << (sig - 1)
}

/// The action's handler word of signal `sig` in the process led by `l`
/// (the area's signal words, kernel/process's `rt_sigaction`).
fn handler_of(l: u64, sig: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: the leader's area, word SIG_WORDS + (sig - 1) * 4 < 512.
    unsafe { ((own_area(l) + (SIG_WORDS + (sig - 1) * 4) * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
}

/// Whether signal `sig`, taken by the process led by `l`, would do
/// nothing: its action is SIG_IGN; or the default, for a signal whose
/// default is to be ignored (SIGCHLD, SIGURG, SIGWINCH) or to continue
/// (SIGCONT, whose continuing is done when it is sent: `job_signal`);
/// or the default at all, for pid 1 (`kill(2)`: init gets only the
/// signals it has a handler for), a stop included. px48: a stop at its
/// default is not inert: it stops the process (`job_signal` when it is
/// sent, kernel/signal when a blocked one is taken).
fn inert(l: u64, sig: u64) -> bool {
    let h = handler_of(l, sig)
    if sig == SIGKILL || sig == SIGSTOP {
        return field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == 1
    }
    if h == SIG_IGN {
        return true
    }
    if h != SIG_DFL {
        return false
    }
    field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == 1 || sig == SIGCHLD || sig == SIGURG || sig == SIGWINCH || sig == SIGCONT
}

/// Whether slot `t` is a thread that can still take a signal.
fn takes(t: u64) -> bool {
    let st = field(t, offset_of(Thread, state))
    st != FREE && st != DEAD && st != ZOMBIE && st != HELD
}

/// The thread of the group led by `l` that takes the process's signal
/// `sig`: the leader when it has it unblocked, else the lowest-numbered
/// thread that has; SLOTS when every thread blocks it.
fn taker(l: u64, sig: u64) -> u64 {
    if takes(l) && field(l, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)) & bit(sig) == 0 {
        return l
    }
    var best: u64 = SLOTS
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        if t != l && field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)) == l && field(t, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) != 0 && takes(t) && field(t, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)) & bit(sig) == 0 {
            if best == SLOTS || field(t, offset_of(Thread, pid)) < field(best, offset_of(Thread, pid)) {
                best = t
            }
        }
        t += 1
    }
    best
}

/// The lowest-numbered signal slot `t` would take now, and whether it
/// is the process's (1) or the thread's own (0); (0, 0) when none.
fn next_for(t: u64) -> (u64, u64) {
    let l = field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    let m = field(t, offset_of(Thread, sigmask))
    let own = field(t, offset_of(Thread, pend)) & !m
    let grp = field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend)) & !m
    if own == 0 && grp == 0 {
        return (0, 0)
    }
    var sig: u64 = 1
    while sig <= NSIG {
        if own & bit(sig) != 0 {
            return (sig, 0)
        }
        if grp & bit(sig) != 0 && taker(l, sig) == t {
            return (sig, 1)
        }
        sig += 1
    }
    (0, 0)
}

/// Make slot `t` ready if it waits in a system call a signal may end
/// (not a vfork parent's wait: `vfork(2)`).
fn interrupt(t: u64) {
    let st = field(t, offset_of(Thread, state))
    if st == INPUT || st == PIPE || st == WAITING || st == SLEEPING || st == FUTEX || st == SUSPEND {
        put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
        enqueue(t)
    }
}

/// Every waiting thread of the group led by `l` that now has a signal
/// to take which will run a handler or end the process is made ready.
/// Called when a signal is raised, a mask changes or a thread ends.
/// IF clear.
fn poke(l: u64) {
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        if field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)) == l && takes(t) && field(t, offset_of(Thread, state)) != READY && field(t, offset_of(Thread, state)) != RUNNING {
            // Any of its deliverable signals that is not inert wakes it.
            let m = field(t, offset_of(Thread, sigmask))
            let own = field(t, offset_of(Thread, pend)) & !m
            let grp = field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend)) & !m
            var sig: u64 = 1
            var hit = false
            while sig <= NSIG && !hit {
                if (own & bit(sig) != 0 || (grp & bit(sig) != 0 && taker(l, sig) == t)) && !inert(l, sig) {
                    hit = true
                }
                sig += 1
            }
            if hit {
                interrupt(t)
            }
        }
        t += 1
    }
}

/// Raise signal `sig` for the thread in slot `t` with origin word
/// `info`: pending for it, unless it would do nothing and the thread
/// does not block it (`signal(7)`: an ignored signal is discarded when
/// it is generated; a blocked one waits, since its action may change).
/// A waiting thread it will reach is made ready. IF clear.
pub fn raise_thread(t: u64, sig: u64, info: u64) {
    if !takes(t) {
        return
    }
    let l = field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    if job_signal(l, sig, field(t, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)) & bit(sig) != 0) {
        return // px48: a stop done now, or one an orphaned group does not take
    }
    if inert(l, sig) && field(t, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)) & bit(sig) == 0 {
        return
    }
    put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, pend), field(t, offset_of(Thread, pend)) | bit(sig))
    put_info(t, 0, sig, info)
    poke(l)
}

/// Raise signal `sig` for the process led by slot `l` (as
/// `raise_thread`; discarded when it would do nothing and some thread
/// has it unblocked).
pub fn raise_group(l: u64, sig: u64, info: u64) {
    if !running_state(l) {
        return
    }
    if job_signal(l, sig, taker(l, sig) == SLOTS) {
        return // px48: a stop done now, or one an orphaned group does not take
    }
    if inert(l, sig) && taker(l, sig) != SLOTS {
        return
    }
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend), field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend)) | bit(sig))
    put_info(l, 1, sig, info)
    poke(l)
}


/// Raise `sig` for the process `pid` (`kill`): false when there is no
/// such process. `sig` 0 only asks.
pub fn raise_pid(pid: u64, sig: u64, info: u64) -> bool {
    let l = live_slot(pid)
    if l == SLOTS {
        return false
    }
    if sig != 0 {
        raise_group(l, sig, info)
    }
    true
}

/// Raise `sig` for the thread `tid` of process `tgid` (`tgkill`; `tgid`
/// 0 for `tkill`: any process's): false when there is no such thread.
pub fn raise_tid(tgid: u64, tid: u64, sig: u64, info: u64) -> bool {
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        if takes(t) && field(t, offset_of(Thread, pid)) == tid && field(t, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) != 0 && (tgid == 0 || field(t, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == tgid) {
            if sig != 0 {
                raise_thread(t, sig, info)
            }
            return true
        }
        t += 1
    }
    false
}

// --- job control (px48) ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// A process's group and session are its leader's `pgid` and `sid`; its
// controlling terminal is the console when its leader's `ctty` is 1 and
// its session is the one the console belongs to (`S_TTY_SID`), whose
// foreground group is `S_TTY_PGRP`. A stopped process is its leader's
// `stopped`; its threads are parked (STOPPED) when next dequeued or when
// they return to ring 3, and readied when it is continued or killed.
// `wstop` and `wcont` are the reports a parent's wait has not taken yet.
// Written from POSIX.1-2024 (XBD 3's process group, session and
// orphaned process group; XBD 11.1.2-11.1.4; XSH setpgid, setsid,
// tcsetpgrp, waitpid, _exit's consequences) and the man pages, against
// black-box runs on Linux (notes/px48-job-control.md); no kernel's source.

const SA_NOCLDSTOP: u64 = 1
const SIGHUP: u64 = 1
const STOPS: u64 = 0x3c0000 // SIGSTOP, SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN, SIGTTOU: bits 18 to 21

fn is_stop(sig: u64) -> bool {
    sig == SIGSTOP || sig == SIGTSTP || sig == SIGTTIN || sig == SIGTTOU
}

fn lw(l: u64, off: int) -> u64 {
    field(l, off)
}

/// Whether slot `l` is a live process's leader: it has a pid, leads its
/// group, and has not ended (a held leader has not).
fn live_leader(l: u64) -> bool {
    let p = field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    p != 0 && field(l, offset_of(Thread, pid)) == p && running_state(l)
}

/// Whether thread `t` is to be parked: its process is stopped.
fn parks(t: u64) -> bool {
    field(t, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) != 0 && field(field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)), offset_of(Thread, stopped)) == 1
}

/// The running thread's process: its group, its session.
pub fn self_pgid() -> u64 {
    lw(field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead)), offset_of(Thread, pgid))
}

pub fn self_sid() -> u64 {
    lw(field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead)), offset_of(Thread, sid))
}

/// Process group and session of the process led by slot `l` (a zombie's
/// too, until it is reaped).
pub fn pgid_of(l: u64) -> u64 {
    lw(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid))
}

pub fn sid_of(l: u64) -> u64 {
    lw(l, offset_of(Thread, sid))
}

/// Whether the process led by slot `l` is stopped.
pub fn is_stopped(l: u64) -> bool {
    lw(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped)) == 1
}

/// Whether some process not yet reaped (a zombie counts) is in group
/// `g`, and (`sid` not 0) in session `sid`.
pub fn group_exists(g: u64, sid: u64) -> bool {
    if g == 0 {
        return false
    }
    var l: u64 = FIRST
    while l < SLOTS {
        if is_proc(l) && field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == g && (sid == 0 || field(l, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == sid) {
            return true
        }
        l += 1
    }
    false
}

/// px48: the members of group `g`, in increasing pid order: the smallest
/// pid above `after` of a process not yet reaped in `g`, 0 when none.
pub fn member_after(g: u64, after: u64) -> u64 {
    var best: u64 = 0
    var l: u64 = FIRST
    while l < SLOTS {
        if is_proc(l) && field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == g {
            let p = field(l, offset_of(Thread, pid))
            if p > after && (best == 0 || p < best) {
                best = p
            }
        }
        l += 1
    }
    best
}

/// `setpgid(pid, pgid)` (pid and pgid already 0-resolved by the caller:
/// `pid` 0 is the caller, `pgid` 0 is `pid`): 0, or the errno (1 EPERM,
/// 3 ESRCH, 13 EACCES). XSH setpgid, measured: the target is the caller
/// or a child of it (else ESRCH); a child that has exec'd is EACCES; a
/// target in another session, or a session leader, is EPERM; a pgid
/// other than the target's pid names a group that must exist in the
/// caller's session (else EPERM).
pub fn setpgid(pid: u64, pgid: u64) -> u64 {
    let me = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead))
    let mypid = field(me, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    let t = if pid == mypid { me } else { live_slot(pid) }
    if t == SLOTS || (t != me && field(t, offset_of(Thread, ppid)) != mypid) {
        return 3
    }
    if t != me && field(t, offset_of(Thread, execd)) == 1 {
        return 13
    }
    let msid = field(me, offset_of(Thread, sid))
    if field(t, offset_of(Thread, sid)) != msid || field(t, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == pid {
        return 1
    }
    if pgid != pid && !group_exists(pgid, msid) {
        return 1
    }
    put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, pgid), pgid)
    0
}

/// `setsid()`: the new session's id (the caller's pid), or 0 when the
/// caller leads a process group (a group with its pid as id exists:
/// EPERM). The caller leads a new session and a new group and has no
/// controlling terminal.
pub fn setsid() -> u64 {
    let me = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead))
    let p = field(me, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    if group_exists(p, 0) {
        return 0
    }
    put_field(me, offset_of(Thread, pgid), p)
    put_field(me, offset_of(Thread, sid), p)
    put_field(me, offset_of(Thread, ctty), 0)
    p
}

/// Whether the process led by slot `l` has the console as its
/// controlling terminal.
pub fn has_tty(l: u64) -> bool {
    let t = sv(S_TTY_SID)
    t != 0 && field(l, offset_of(Thread, ctty)) == 1 && field(l, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == t
}

/// Whether the running thread's process has the console as its
/// controlling terminal.
pub fn self_tty() -> bool {
    field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, tgid)) != 0 && has_tty(field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead)))
}

/// kernel/console's `start`: the console is a terminal from here, which
/// the next process the kernel starts takes (`start_thread`).
pub fn tty_on() {
    put_sv(S_TTY_ON, 1)
}

/// The console's session (0: no session holds it) and foreground group
/// (0: none).
pub fn tty_session() -> u64 {
    sv(S_TTY_SID)
}

pub fn tty_pgrp() -> u64 {
    sv(S_TTY_PGRP)
}

pub fn set_tty_pgrp(g: u64) {
    put_sv(S_TTY_PGRP, g)
}

/// The console leaves session `sid`: no process of it has the console
/// as its controlling terminal any more (XBD 11.1.3).
fn tty_leaves(sid: u64) {
    if sv(S_TTY_SID) != sid {
        return
    }
    put_sv(S_TTY_SID, 0)
    put_sv(S_TTY_PGRP, 0)
    var l: u64 = FIRST
    while l < SLOTS {
        if field(l, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == sid {
            put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, ctty), 0)
        }
        l += 1
    }
}

/// TIOCSCTTY by the running thread's process (`steal`: arg 1 by a
/// privileged caller): 0 or the errno (1 EPERM). A session leader with no
/// controlling terminal takes the console when no session holds it, or
/// (`steal`) from the session that does, whose foreground group is sent
/// SIGHUP and SIGCONT (measured: the group heard SIGHUP); its own group
/// is the console's foreground group. Its own terminal: 0.
pub fn take_tty(steal: bool) -> u64 {
    let me = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead))
    let p = field(me, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    if has_tty(me) {
        return 0
    }
    if field(me, offset_of(Thread, sid)) != p {
        return 1
    }
    let old = sv(S_TTY_SID)
    if old != 0 {
        if !steal {
            return 1
        }
        let fg = sv(S_TTY_PGRP)
        tty_leaves(old)
        hangup_group(fg, true)
    }
    put_sv(S_TTY_SID, p)
    put_sv(S_TTY_PGRP, field(me, offset_of(Thread, pgid)))
    put_field(me, offset_of(Thread, ctty), 1)
    0
}

/// A session leader with no controlling terminal opens the console
/// (without O_NOCTTY, kernel/files): it takes it when no session holds
/// it (XBD 11.1.3: the first terminal a session leader opens).
pub fn open_tty() {
    let me = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead))
    let p = field(me, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    if p != 0 && sv(S_TTY_ON) == 1 && sv(S_TTY_SID) == 0 && field(me, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == p {
        let _r = take_tty(false)
    }
}

/// TIOCNOTTY by the running thread's process: 0, or 25 (ENOTTY) when
/// the console is not its controlling terminal. A session leader's
/// session loses it and the foreground group is sent SIGHUP and
/// SIGCONT (measured); any other process loses it alone.
pub fn drop_tty() -> u64 {
    let me = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead))
    if !has_tty(me) {
        return 25
    }
    let p = field(me, offset_of(Thread, tgid))
    if field(me, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == p {
        let fg = sv(S_TTY_PGRP)
        tty_leaves(p)
        hangup_group(fg, true)
    } else {
        put_field(me, offset_of(Thread, ctty), 0)
    }
    0
}

/// SIGHUP, and (`cont`) SIGCONT, for every process of group `g`, from
/// the kernel (no permission asked).
fn hangup_group(g: u64, cont: bool) {
    if g == 0 {
        return
    }
    let info = sig_info(I_KERNEL, 0, 0, 0)
    let _n = raise_pgrp(g, SIGHUP, info)
    if cont {
        let _m = raise_pgrp(g, SIGCONT, info)
    }
}

/// Raise `sig` for every live process of group `g` (the terminal's
/// keys, its background rules, a hangup): how many there were.
pub fn raise_pgrp(g: u64, sig: u64, info: u64) -> u64 {
    var n: u64 = 0
    var l: u64 = FIRST
    while l < SLOTS {
        if live_leader(l) && field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == g {
            n += 1
            if sig != 0 {
                raise_group(l, sig, info)
            }
        }
        l += 1
    }
    n
}

/// Whether group `g` is orphaned (XBD 3): no live member but `skip` (a
/// slot; SLOTS none) has a parent, other than `skip`, in another group
/// of the same session. A group with no live member is orphaned.
fn orphaned_but(g: u64, skip: u64) -> bool {
    var l: u64 = FIRST
    while l < SLOTS {
        if l != skip && live_leader(l) && field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == g {
            let pp = field(l, offset_of(Thread, ppid))
            let ps = if pp != 0 { live_slot(pp) } else { SLOTS }
            if ps != SLOTS && ps != skip && field(ps, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) != g && field(ps, offset_of(Thread, sid)) == field(l, offset_of(Thread, sid)) {
                return false
            }
        }
        l += 1
    }
    true
}

/// Whether the running thread's process group is orphaned.
pub fn self_orphaned() -> bool {
    orphaned_but(self_pgid(), SLOTS)
}

/// Whether group `g` has a stopped live member.
fn has_stopped(g: u64) -> bool {
    var l: u64 = FIRST
    while l < SLOTS {
        if live_leader(l) && field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)) == g && field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped)) == 1 {
            return true
        }
        l += 1
    }
    false
}

/// Whether the running thread blocks `sig` or its process ignores it
/// (the terminal's background rules: then a read is -EIO and a write
/// goes ahead).
pub fn self_refuses(sig: u64) -> bool {
    let c = cur()
    field(c, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)) & bit(sig) != 0 || handler_of(field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead)), sig) == SIG_IGN
}

/// The parent of the process led by `l` is told of a stop or a continue
/// (`kind` I_CHLD_STOPPED or I_CHLD_CONTINUED, `sig` the signal): its
/// waiting threads are woken, and it is sent SIGCHLD unless its action
/// for SIGCHLD has SA_NOCLDSTOP (sigaction(2), measured both ways).
fn tell_parent(l: u64, kind: u64, sig: u64) {
    let pp = field(l, offset_of(Thread, ppid))
    let ps = if pp != 0 { live_slot(pp) } else { SLOTS }
    if ps == SLOTS {
        return
    }
    wake_waiter(pp)
    // # Safety: the parent's area, word SIG_WORDS + 16 * 4 + 1 < 512: its SIGCHLD action's flags.
    let fl = unsafe { ((own_area(ps) + (SIG_WORDS + (SIGCHLD - 1) * 4 + 1) * 8) as *u64).read_volatile() }
    if fl & SA_NOCLDSTOP == 0 {
        raise_group(ps, SIGCHLD, sig_info(kind, field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)), sig, real_uid(l)))
    }
}

/// Stop the process led by `l` with `sig`: it runs nothing until
/// continued; its parent is told. A stopped process stopped again is
/// told nothing new.
fn stop_proc(l: u64, sig: u64) {
    if field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped)) == 1 {
        return
    }
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped), 1)
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, wstop), sig)
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, wcont), 0)
    tell_parent(l, I_CHLD_STOPPED, sig)
}

/// Every parked thread of the process led by `l` made ready.
fn unpark(l: u64) {
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        if field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)) == l && field(t, offset_of(Thread, state)) == STOPPED {
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, state), READY)
            enqueue(t)
        }
        t += 1
    }
}

/// The stop signals pending for the process led by `l`, or (`cont`)
/// its SIGCONT, discarded (signal(7): a SIGCONT discards a pending stop,
/// a stop a pending SIGCONT).
fn discard(l: u64, cont: bool) {
    let m = if cont { bit(SIGCONT) } else { STOPS }
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend), field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend)) & !m)
    var t: u64 = FIRST
    while t < SLOTS {
        if field(t, offset_of(Thread, lead)) == l && takes(t) {
            put_field(t, offset_of(Thread, pend), field(t, offset_of(Thread, pend)) & !m)
        }
        t += 1
    }
}

/// What a signal does when it is sent, before it is made pending (px48):
/// true when that is all it does. SIGKILL readies a stopped process's
/// threads so it can end. SIGCONT continues a stopped process whatever
/// its action (blocked or ignored too) and discards a pending stop; the
/// parent is told. A stop discards a pending SIGCONT; at its default
/// (SIGSTOP always), when some thread takes it now (`blocked` false), it
/// stops the process at once — or, for SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN and SIGTTOU, is
/// discarded when the process's group is orphaned (XBD 11.1.4) — and is
/// not made pending. pid 1 is never stopped (`inert`).
fn job_signal(l: u64, sig: u64, blocked: bool) -> bool {
    if field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == 1 {
        return false
    }
    if sig == SIGKILL {
        if field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped)) == 1 {
            put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped), 0)
            unpark(l)
        }
        return false
    }
    if sig == SIGCONT {
        discard(l, false)
        continue_proc(l)
        return false
    }
    if !is_stop(sig) {
        return false
    }
    discard(l, true)
    if sig != SIGSTOP && (handler_of(l, sig) != SIG_DFL || blocked) {
        return false
    }
    if sig != SIGSTOP && orphaned_but(field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)), SLOTS) {
        return true
    }
    stop_proc(l, sig)
    true
}

/// The process led by `l`, stopped, continued: its threads ready again,
/// its parent told.
fn continue_proc(l: u64) {
    if field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped)) == 0 {
        return
    }
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, stopped), 0)
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, wstop), 0)
    put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, wcont), 1)
    unpark(l)
    tell_parent(l, I_CHLD_CONTINUED, SIGCONT)
}

/// kernel/signal: the running thread took stop signal `sig` at its
/// default (one that was blocked when it was sent): its process stops,
/// unless it is a SIGTSTP, SIGTTIN or SIGTTOU and its group is orphaned
/// (then the signal is discarded).
pub fn stop_self(sig: u64) {
    let l = field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead))
    if field(l, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == 1 {
        return
    }
    if sig != SIGSTOP && orphaned_but(field(l, offset_of(Thread, pgid)), SLOTS) {
        return
    }
    stop_proc(l, sig)
}

/// Whether the running thread is to be parked now (its process is
/// stopped): kernel/signal asks before it takes a signal.
pub fn self_parks() -> bool {
    parks(cur())
}

/// Park the running thread (its process is stopped) with its frame
/// `f`, which it resumes when continued; the next thread's frame. IF
/// clear.
pub fn park_current(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), STOPPED)
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px48: the running thread's system call, rewound to run again, is to
/// be ended by a signal's handler as a waiting call is (W_REWOUND: run
/// again under SA_RESTART, else -EINTR): the terminal's background rule
/// sent its group SIGTTIN or SIGTTOU (kernel/console).
pub fn self_rewound() {
    put_field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, rst), W_REWOUND)
}

/// px48: `rt_sigsuspend(mask)` from the frame `f` (sigsuspend(2)): the
/// running thread's mask is `mask` (SIGKILL and SIGSTOP never blocked)
/// until a signal comes that runs a handler or ends the process; the
/// call then answers -EINTR, and the mask it replaced is put back by the
/// handler's frame (kernel/signal gives it that mask to restore); px49:
/// a thread that comes out with no handler run waits again
/// (`resuspend`), and `pause` (pax#73) is this with the caller's own
/// mask. The next thread's frame. IF clear.
pub fn suspend_current(f: u64, mask: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    // # Safety: the caller's live frame (22 words).
    unsafe { ((f + W_RAX * 8) as *u64).write_volatile(0xfffffffffffffffc) } // -EINTR
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, ssold), field(c, offset_of(Thread, sigmask)))
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, sigmask), mask & !0x40100)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), SUSPEND)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst), W_SUSPEND)
    poke(field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead))) // a signal already pending and now unblocked ends it at once
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// px48: whether the running thread is leaving `rt_sigsuspend`, and the
/// mask it is to have back.
pub fn self_suspended() -> (bool, u64) {
    let c = cur()
    (field(c, offset_of(Thread, rst)) == W_SUSPEND, field(c, offset_of(Thread, ssold)))
}

/// px49 (pax#75): the running thread is on its way back to ring 3 from
/// `rt_sigsuspend` (or `pause`) with no handler run for it — it took a
/// stop at its default and was continued, or it was woken for a process
/// signal another thread took first — and its process is not ending: it
/// waits again with the same mask and the same mask to put back, as
/// Linux keeps it waiting (measured: notes/px49-the-small-gaps.md §B).
/// px48 let it go with -EINTR. The next thread's frame. IF clear.
pub fn resuspend(f: u64) -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, frame), f)
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, state), SUSPEND)
    poke(field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead))) // a signal deliverable now ends it at once
    wake_sleepers()
    run_next()
}

/// The session of the process whose area is `a` (a leader's own area,
/// `proc_area`'s; kernel/signal's `may_signal` has areas).
pub fn sid_of_area(a: u64) -> u64 {
    // # Safety: the address only.
    let base = unsafe { pax_procs as u64 }
    if a < base || a >= base + SLOTS * PROC_AREA {
        return 0
    }
    field((a - base) / PROC_AREA, offset_of(Thread, sid))
}

/// Whether the running thread has a signal to take now (kernel/signal
/// asks on every return to ring 3). False before `start`.
pub fn signal_waits() -> bool {
    if sv(S_STARTED) == 0 {
        return false
    }
    let c = cur()
    if field(c, offset_of(Thread, tgid)) == 0 {
        return false
    }
    let (sig, _g) = next_for(c)
    sig != 0
}

/// Take the running thread's next signal: (its number, its origin
/// word); (0, 0) when none. The lowest-numbered first.
pub fn take_signal() -> (u64, u64) {
    let c = cur()
    let l = field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead))
    let (sig, g) = next_for(c)
    if sig == 0 {
        return (0, 0)
    }
    if g == 1 {
        put_field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend), field(l, offset_of(Thread, gpend)) & !bit(sig))
        return (sig, info_of(l, 1, sig))
    }
    put_field(c, offset_of(Thread, pend), field(c, offset_of(Thread, pend)) & !bit(sig))
    (sig, info_of(c, 0, sig))
}

/// The signals pending for the running thread, its own and its
/// process's (`rt_sigpending`).
pub fn self_pending() -> u64 {
    let c = cur()
    field(c, offset_of(Thread, pend)) | field(field(c, offset_of(Thread, lead)), offset_of(Thread, gpend))
}

/// The running thread's mask has changed, or an action has: a signal of
/// the process may be another thread's to take now.
pub fn masks_changed() {
    poke(field(cur(), offset_of(Thread, lead)))
}

/// A signal was delivered to a handler (kernel/signal counts them for
/// its census line).
pub fn count_signal() -> u64 {
    let n = sv(S_SIGNALS) + 1
    put_sv(S_SIGNALS, n)
    n
}

/// The scheduler's line:
/// `sched: main 0, idle 1, 64 slots, stacks 16 KiB above a 48 KiB guard from 0x<STACKS>, quantum 2 ticks`.
pub fn report() {
    log.put("sched: main 0, idle 1, ")
    log.dec(SLOTS as int)
    log.put(" slots, stacks ")
    log.dec((STACK / 1024) as int)
    log.put(" KiB above a ")
    log.dec((GUARD / 1024) as int)
    log.put(" KiB guard from ")
    log.hex(STACKS)
    log.put(", quantum ")
    log.dec(QUANTUM as int)
    log.put(" ticks")
    log.end()
}
